Welsh National Assembly members feare to be called muppets
Among some members of the National Assembly there was consternation. This is the result of a proposal to change their title as members of the Welsh Parliament.
The idea arose in connection with plans to rename the assembly to the Welsh Parliament.
Assembly members from all sides of the political scene are concerned that they may become the subject of mockery.
One of the Labour MPs voiced concerns of his party, saying the new name rhymes with the Welsh words "twp" and "pwp."
For non-Waless readers: in Welsh, the word “twp” means a fool, and the word “pwp” – feces.
One Plaid Cymru politician stated that his party was "dissatisfied" with the proposals and presented other solutions.
A member of the Welsh Conservative Party said that his party is “opened” to change its name. However, he pointed out that the word “MWP” is very similar to the word “muppet”.
In this case, the Welsh letter “w” is pronounced similarly to the letter ‘u’ in the English dialect Yorkshire.
The assembly committee, which is currently working on a draft law changing its name, took the following position: "A naturally final decision on the future title of the members of the assembly will belong to themselves."
The Government of Wales Act 2017 gave the Welsh National Assembly the right to change its name.
In June, the committee published the results of a public consultation on the proposal. Renaming the assembly into the Welsh Parliament gained widespread support.
In addition, the committee backed a proposal to change the title of the members of the assembly to “Member of the Welsh Parliament”, i.e. WMP in English, although the abbreviation “MWP” enjoyed the greatest popularity among the public.
Assembly members are proposing alternative solutions. Difficulties in reaching a consensus are a nuisance for Elin Jones, chairman of the assembly, which in the coming weeks should present the bill.
The law introducing reforms will also take into account other changes in the manner of work of the assembly, including the rules for dismissing members and the structure of the comitology system.
Members of the National Assembly will be able to vote on their future title when discussing the bill.
Macodonians vote in referendum to change the country's name
Next Sunday, the voters will decide whether their country will soon bear the name of the Republic of North Macedonia.
The popular vote is intended to help avert a decades-long dispute with neighboring Greece, which houses a region called Macedonia.
The government in Athens has long argued that the name of their northern neighbor constitutes a territorial claim, and has repeatedly opposed attempts to join the European Union and NATO.
Ǵorge Ivanov, the president of Macedonia and an opponent of a referendum on changing the name, said he would disregard the results of the vote.
However, supporters of the plebiscite, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaew, maintain that change is necessary to enable the state to join the European Union and NATO.
Bells in New York's St. Martin's Church are silent about problems afflicting churches in Harlem
“Older people I talk to say that in the past on every corner you could find a bar and a church,” Henry Adams said.
“Currently, there is no trace of them.”
As he says, the disappearance of bars is understandable.
“Currently, people are sociable in a different way,” he noted.
“Bars have ceased to be a place for regular meetings of members of the community.”
In the case of churches, however, he is worried that the funds from the sale of property will not be enough for as long as the decision-makers believe, and that “sooner or later they will be at the starting point.”
He added that churches could be replaced by apartment buildings inhabited by people who would not support the remaining surrounding temples.
As he said: “The overwhelming majority of people buying flats in such buildings will be white. This will accelerate the complete closure of churches, because it is unlikely that new residents will become members of these parishes.”
Both churches were built by a gathering of white faithful, even before Harlem joined the metropolitan community in 1870 (the church of St. Martin joined the community a decade later).
The original gathering of white Methodists left the district in the 1930s.
At that time, the surrounding Afro-American assembly seized ownership of the building.
St. Martin's Church was seized by an assembly chaired by Reverend John Howard Johnson who led a boycott against sellers from the 125th Street (Harlem's main shopping street), opposing hiring or promoting African-Americans.
The 1939 fire significantly damaged the building, but the parishioners of John Howard Johnson made plans to rebuild the temple and ordered the construction of a bell assembly.
Reverend David Johnson, the son of John Howard Johnson, and his successor at St. Martin's Church, proudly called the new carylion "bells of the poor."
The expert, which struck the bells in July, described them in a different way as "a cultural treasure" and an "irreplaceable historical instrument."
Tiffany Ng, an expert from the University of Michigan, also noted that this is the world’s first carillion operated by a black musician. This artist was Dionisio A. Lind, who 18 years ago moved to Riverside Church, where a larger band was waiting for him.
Malcolm J. Merriweather said that the church of St. Martin did not replace him.
In recent months there have been a number of complicated events involving architects and contractors in the church of St. Martin. Some of them were brought to the place by lay leaders of the church, and some – the diocese of the Episcopal Church.
In July, the parish council (the governing body of a parish composed of secular leaders) addressed its concerns to the diocese, claiming that it "wants to shift the costs to the council," although the council did not take part in hiring architects and contractors sent to the site.
Some parishioners also complain about the lack of transparency on the part of the diocese.
A shark injured a 13-year-old diving in search of lobsters in California
Authorities reported that last Saturday, a shark attacked and injured a 13-year-old diving in search of lobsters in California. The incident happened on the first day of the lobster season.
The attack took place just before 7 a.m. near Beacon’s Beach in the U.S. city of Encinitas.
Chad Hammel reported to KSWB-TV in San Diego that on Saturday morning for about half an hour he was diving with friends when he heard a cry for help. Immediately with a group of acquaintances, they approached the boy to help pull him out of the water.
As Hammel said, at first it seemed to him that the boy was excited to catch the lobster. He soon realized, however, that the child was screaming: “I was bitten!
"I've been bitten!"
Upon reaching the scene, Chad Hammel noticed that the boy had completely torn skin in the collarbone area.
“I started shouting at everyone to escape from the water: ‘There’s a shark in the water,’” Hammel added.
The boy was transported by helicopter to the Children’s Hospital Children’s Hospital in San Diego. His condition was described as critical.
The species of shark responsible for the attack remains unknown.
During a news conference paramedic Captain Larry Giles said the fish had already been spotted a few weeks earlier. However, it was acknowledged that it did not belong to a species of menacing sharks.
Larry Giles added that the victim suffered serious injuries to the upper torso.
In order to carry out an investigation and for security reasons, the authorities for 48 hours closed the entrance to the beach from Ponto Beach in Casablad, up to Swami Beach in Ecinitas.
Larry Giles noted that more than 135 species of sharks lived in the area, although most of them are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury’s chain of stores plans to expand its operations in the British cosmetics market
Sainsbury's chain throws down glosses to stores such as Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams, introducing cosmetic departments referring in style to department stores, as well as specially trained retailers.
As part of a significant investment in the British cosmetics market – which is worth 2.8 billion pounds and is constantly developing despite declines in the fashion industry and household goods – there will be larger racks with cosmetic products in 11 stores owned by the chain. If the initiative succeeds next year, activities will be extended to the next points.
The return to the cosmetics industry stems from the search for ways of developing the space previously occupied by televisions, microwaves and household items.
According to Sainsbury’s, the network will double the number of cosmetic products and offer up to 3,000 articles. For the first time, goods from brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman or Dr. PawPaw will appear in the assortment for the first time.
The currently sold L’Oreal, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees products will also receive more space and separate areas similar to those known even from Boots stores.
In addition, the supermarket chain plans to refresh its range of make-up products Boutique. From now on, most of the articles will be vegan-friendly, which younger customers are increasingly demanding.
In addition to this, perfume retailer Fragrance Shop will create test points at two Sainsbury’s supermarkets. The first opened last week in Croydon, south London, and the second will be built in Selly Oak, Birmingham, later in the year.
The prevalency of online sales and the daily shopping of small amounts of food in local stores means that supermarkets need to encourage customers to visit more.
Mike Coupe, president of Sainsbury's, said outlets would increasingly resemble department stores as the network tries to fight discounters such as Aldi or Lidl, offering more services and non-food products.
Sainsbury’s has placed Argos outlets in hundreds of stores and has been introducing a number of Habitat outlets since the acquisition of both companies two years ago. According to the company, this contributes to increasing grocery sales and an increase in investment profitability.
The previous attempt to reorganize departments with cosmetic products and pharmaceuticals ended in failure.
At the beginning of the 21st century, Sainsbury’s created a joint-venture company from Boots. However, the cooperation ended in the aftermath of the conflict over the distribution of revenues from pharmacies operating in supermarkets.
Sainsbury’s introduces a new strategy in the aftermath of the sale of 281 pharmacies to Celesio, the owner of the Lloyds Pharmacy chain, for 125 million pounds three years ago.
According to reports, Lloyds will contribute to the implementation of the plan, introducing an expanded range of luxury brands for skin care, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy, to the chain's four stores.
Paul Mills-Hicks, Sainsbury’s commercial director, said: “We’ve transformed the appearance and nature of cosmetics departments to improve customer experiences.
We've invested in special training for co-workers so they can help consumers when shopping.
Our brands are able to meet all needs, and the friendly environment and convenient locations allow us to attract the attention of customers looking for cosmetic products, as well as to question the existing ways of shopping.”
Peter Jones 'furious' after Holly Willoughby pulls out of contract worth 11 million pounds
Peet Jones, the star of Dragons' Den, was "furious" when he learned that Holly Willoughby intends to pull out of a 11 million pound lifestyle brand to focus on a renewed partnership with Marks and Spencer and ITV
Holly Willoughby doesn't have time for their brand offering accessories and household goods called Truly.
The activity was often compared to the Goop brand owned by Gwyneth Paltrow.
This morning, the 37-year-old presenter announced her departure via Instagram.
A sudden decision to leave a thriving lifestyle brand and focus on a renewed partnership with Marks & Spencer and ITV undertaken by Holly Willoughby infuriated Peter Jones, star of Dragons’ Den.
According to sources, Peter Jones was "furious" when a popular television presenter announced during a nervous Tuesday meeting at the headquarters of his business empire in Marlow, Buckinghamshire that due to new contracts worth up to 1.5 million pounds she would not have time to devote herself to their brand offering Truly accessories and household items.
The company was previously compared to Gwyneth Paltrow’s Goop brand. It was also supposed to double Holly Willoughby’s fortune estimated at 11 million pounds.
After announcing his decision to leave the Truly brand by 37-year-old Holly Willoughby on Instagram, Peter Jones left the UK, going to one of his holiday homes.
As the source said: "Truly's brand was a priority for Holly Willoughby.
She had her future with her and wanted to deal with her for the next several decades.
The decision to withdraw from the activity completely surprised all those involved.
No one could believe what happened on Tuesday. It was already so close to entering the market.
There are ready-to-sell goods in Marlow Warehouse.”
Experts believe the resignation of the presenter of This Morning and one of Britain's most successful stars could cost the company millions of pounds due to significant investments into products -- including items ranging from pillows through candles to clothing and household items - and will also lead to a further delay of entry into the market.
It can also mean the end of long-lasting friendship.
Holly Willoughby (mother of three children) and her husband Dan Baldwin have had a close relationship with Peter Jones and his wife Tara Capp for ten years.
Holly Willoughby and Tara Capp founded the Truly brand in 2016, and Peter Jones, 52-year-old, joined them as president in March.
The couples spent their holidays together, with Jones taking a 40% stake in a television production company owned by Dan Baldwin.
Holly Willoughby is expected to become an M&S brand ambassador and will replace Ant McPartlin as the host of I'm A Celebrity on ITV.
A source close to Peter Jones said last night: "We won't be commenting on his business affairs."
Difficult conversation, “and then we fell in love with each other”
The president joked about criticism of the media caused by the remembrance that some people deemed "inconvenient to the statesman," as well as a positive relationship with North Korea's leader.
Why did President Trump give up so much?
Trump said with a mocking voice, pretending to be a TV presenter:
“I didn’t give up anything.”
The president pointed out that Kim Jong-un is interested in the second meeting. The first meeting took place in Singapore in June and was hailed by Trump as a big step towards the denuclearization of North Korea.
However, negotiations on nuclear disarmament are being delayed.
More than three months after the June summit in Singapore, North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho told world leaders at the U.N. General Assembly on Saturday that he sees no "analogous response" from the United States to North Korea's early efforts to disarm.
He noted that instead the United States upholders sanctions and pressuring.
President Trump expressed a much more optimistic view in his speech.
"We're doing great with North Korea," he said.
“Were planning a war with North Korea.
Millions of people would have died.
Now we have a wonderful relationship.”
The president said his efforts to improve relations with Kim Jong-un had a positive effect: they led to an end to missile tests, helped free the hostages and returned the remaining U.S. soldiers home.
In addition, he defended his unusual approach while discussing relations with Kim Jong-un.
“It’s easy to behave presidentially. However, instead of 10,000 people waiting outside and trying to get to a crowded stadium, we’d have 200 people here,” Trump said, pointinging his finger at the crowd gathered directly in front of them.
A tsunami and an earthquake in Indonesia completely destroyed one of the islands, killing hundreds of people
After an earthquake on Lombok, foreign NGOs were told that their help was not needed.
Despite the loss of a roof over the head by more than 10% of Lombok residents, the authorities did not declare a natural disaster, which is a prerequisite for speeding up the activities of international aid.
“Unfortunately, in many cases, the authorities have made it very clear that they are not demanding international support. The situation is therefore difficult,” said Selina Sumbung.
Although Save the Children are preparing to travel to Palu's Indonesian city, it is not yet certain whether international staff will be able to operate at the scene.
Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, a spokesperson for the national disaster agency, said the Indonesian authorities were assessing the situation in Palu to see if assistance from international organizations should be allowed.
Faced by the tremors constantly felt in Indonesia, the country remains completely unprepared to deal with the forces of nature.
Although tsunami shelters have been built in Aceh province, they are rare for the rest of the coastline.
The absence of a siren warning about the Palu tsunami – despite issuing a warning – was likely to have an impact on the death toll.
Even in the most favorable conditions, traveling between Indonesian islands is a challenge.
Natural disasters make logistics even harder.
A hospital ship that was stationed on Lombok Island is headed to help the victims of the quake into the city of Palu. However, it will take at least three days to reach your destination.
President Joko Widodo has made an improvement in Indonesian infrastructure the main point of his campaign and has allocated plenty of funds to roads and railways.
However, in the face of next year’s elections, the president’s administration is struggling with a lack of resources.
In addition, Joko Widodo came under pressure due to persisting religious tensions in the country. Members of the majority Muslim community adopted a more conservative form of faith.
More than 1,000 people have died, and tens of thousands of residents have lost a roof over their heads as a result of fights between Christian and Muslim gangs using machetes, bows, arrows and other straight weapons on the streets.
Look: Liverpool player Daniel Sturridge scores goal to draw against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge rescued Liverpool from losing Chelsea in the English Premier League, scoringing in the 89th minute of Saturday's match at Stamford Beat in London.
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri at a distance of about 27 yards from Chelsea's goal at 1:0 to the detriment of his team.
Before taking a shot in the direction of a distant goal, he moved the ball to his left leg.
The ball soared high above the penalty area towards the upper right corner of the net.
She then fell into the goal, flying over Kepa Arrizabalaga.
“I just tried to take the position and take the ball, knowing that players like Shaqiri are always trying to move it forward as much as possible. I wanted to give myself the best opportunity to shot,” Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
“I noticed Kante approaching, accepted the ball, and gave the shot away without thinking more deeply.”
Chelsea led 1:0 in the middle of the match after the goal of Belgian star Eden Hazard scored in the 25th minute.
The Blues striker kicked the ball with his heel to Mateo Kovačić and then ran towards Liverpool's half.
Kovačić quickly accepted the pass in the middle of the pitch.
He then made a beautiful play forward into the penalty area back to Hazard.
Hazard overtook the defenders and with his left's foot fired a shot towards the long post, which passed Liverpool goalkeeper Alisson Becker.
Liverpool will face Napoli in the Champions League group stage on Wednesday at 15:00 at Stadio San Paolo in Naples (Italy).
Chelsea will face Videoton in the UEFA Europa League on Thursday at 15:00 in London.
The death toll from Indonesia's tsunami has risen to 832 people
Indonesia’s Disaster Agency said on Sunday morning, the death toll from the country’s recent earthquake and tsunami has risen to 832 people.
During the conference, Sutopo spokesman Purwo Nugroho reported that as a result of Friday’s 7.5-degree quake, many people were trapped under debris. Additionally, the tremor triggered a tsunami wave 6 meters high.
Inhabited by more than 380 thousand people, the citity of Palu is full of rubbles of collapsed buildings.
Police have arrested a 32-year-old man suspected of stabbing the woman
After finding the woman's body this morning in Birkenhead, Merseyside County, an investigation into the killing was launched.
A 44-year-old woman was found at 7:55 a.m. with stab wounds at Grayson Mews on John Street. A 32-year-old man was arrested.
Police are asking for contact from people in the area who have seen or heard any suspicious incident.
Brian O'Hagan, a commissioner from the Investigation Division, said: "The investigation is at an early stage, but I would like to call for contact with all those who were near John Street in Birkenhead at the time who saw or heard anything suspicious.
I am also appealing to all drivers, and in particular to taxi drivers whose recorders may have consolidated materials useful during the investigation.”
A police spokesman confirmed that the woman was inhabiting Birkenhead and that her body had been found inside the property.
In the afternoon, those claiming to know the woman arrived at the scene to find out where she was found.
Police said they were in the process of informing the victim's family about the incident.
A taxi driver living in Grayson Mews recently attempted to get into an apartment building. However, he was informed by police that entry to the facility and its abandonment is currently prohibited.
He disappeared when he found out what had happened.
Residents have just been informed that they will not be able to return to their apartments in the near future.
It was heard that one of the officers explained to the man that the area was currently believed to be a crime scene.
A weeping woman appeared at the scene.
She didn’t stop saying, “It’s sorry.”
At 14:00 there were two police cars on the fenced-off area, and another police vehicle was parked next to it.
There are officers guarding the apartment building inside the separated zone.
Persons with information are asked to be contacted via Twitter (@MerPolCC) or by phone at 101, or to report anonymously via Crimestoppers at 0800 555 111, specifyinging event number 247 on September 30.
Cromwell's monument to the British parliament's latest casualty in dispute over 're-writing history'
Removing the monument would bring justice for the Taliban's reminiscent destruction of many English cultural and religious monuments by its radical, puritan supporters.
However, the Cromwell Society described Jeremy Crick’s proposal as “madness” and “an attempt to rewrite history.”
John Goldsmith, president of the Cromwell Society, said: "Given the ongoing debate over the removal of monuments, it was a matter of time for Oliver Cromwell in front of the Westminster Palace.
The illucostive actions in the course of English civil wars were neither commissioned nor executed by Cromwell.
Perhaps for the actions of his ancestor Thomas centuries earlier it was decided to punish not this Cromwell.
The way Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft introduced Cromwell at the monument testifies to the reputation of this politician in the 19th century and that for many people he is a character still worth commemorating.
John Goldsmith told The Sunday Telegraph: "Cromwell's many people were seen - perhaps more often in the 19th century than today - as a defender of parliament against external pressures. In this case, of course, it was about the monarchy.
The assessment of how accurate this statement is is the subject of constant historical debate.
However, it is certain that the conflict of the mid-17th century shaped the further development of our nation, and Cromwell is a recognizable figure identifying one of the parties to the division.
His accomplishments as lord of protector also deserve to be commemorated and commemorated.”
There has been a deadly slaughter of a farmer by a pig in China
According to local media reports, there has been a deadly pig attack on a farmer in a marketplace in southwestern China.
On Sunday, the South China Morning Post newspaper reported that a dead man named Yuan had been fouled with a bloodied and slit artery near a pigsty on a market in the town of Liupanshui, Kuejczou province.
A pig breeder prepares to carry out vaccinations in a piggy store on May 30, 2005, the city of Xining, in Qinghai (China) province.
According to reports, the man arrived at the market with his cousin from the neighboring Junnan province to sell 15 pigs.
The next morning, his cousin found the man's body and noticed an open door to the nearby pigsty.
He said there was a large piglet in the pigsty with blood on his face.
The autopsy confirmed that it was a nearly 250-kilogram pig who fatally beaten the farmer.
"My cousin's legs were bleeded and messed up," a man named Wu was quoted as saying by Guiyang Evening News.
The CCTV footage shows Yuan arrived at the market on Thursday at 4:40 a.m. to feed the pigs.
His body was found about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man didn't belong to Yuan or his cousin.
The market manager told Guiyang Evening News in an interview that for the duration of the collection of evidence by the police, the piglet was isolated to avoid another attack.
According to reports, the man's family and the market authorities are negotiating compensation for death.
There have been cases of swine attacks on humans before, although they are rare.
In 2016, a pig assaulted a woman and her husband on a Massachusetts farm. The man suffered fatal wounds.
Ten years earlier in Wales, a nearly 300-kilogram pig nailed a farmer to a tractor. The man's wife managed to pull the animal away.
After being eated by an Oregon farmer by pigs in 2012, one farmer in Manitoba told CBC News that these animals are not usually aggressive, but the taste of blood can unleash violent behavior.
“They just want to have fun.
They're like an inquisitive child... they don't want to hurt anyone.
It's just that you have to show them the right respect," he said.
Hurricane-residues Rosa will result in heavy rainfall in the southwestern U.S
Hurricane Rosa is losing its strength by moving over the cooler waters of Mexico's northern coast.
In the coming days, however, it will cause heavy rainfall in northern Mexico and the south-western United States.
Hurricane Rosa blows at a speed of almost 137 km/h. On Sunday, at 5:00 a.m. of eastern time, it was given a category 1. It was then located 620 km southwest of Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
According to forecasts on Sunday, Rosa will move to the north.
In the meantime, over the Pacific Ocean, it is beginning to form rather than heading east towards the west coast of the United States. On Monday, Rosa will approach the California Peninsula in the form of a tropical storm, moving masses of humid air north to the southwestern United States.
On Monday, it will bring precipitation up to 25 cm high on part of the territory of Mexico.
In the following days, tropical humid air coming into contact with the impending low will cause heavy rainfall in the U.S
In some places, precipitations ranging in height from 2.5 to 10 cm will lead to sudden floods, the movement of pollution and possible landslides in desert areas.
Moist tropical air will cause precipitation from 5 to 7.5 cm per hour. This is especially the case for parts of Nevada and Arizona.
Even 5 to 10 cm of rain is predicted in some areas of the U.S
Due to the dispersed nature of tropical precipitation, it is possible to cause sudden floods and rapid changes in conditions.
In the face of the threat of tropical rain, it is strongly discouraged to move on foot in the desert area.
Abundant downpours can turn ducks into rushing rivers, and thunderstorms will cause local gusty winds and dust displacement.
Coming than will bring heavy local rainfall on the coast of Southern California.
Rains with a total height of more than 1.3 cm are possible, which may cause small displacements of pollution and danger on the roads.
This would be the first rainfall in the region.
Passing tropical rains will begin to approach Arizona on late Sunday and early Monday. On Monday afternoon and Tuesday, precipitation will occur in more areas.
On Tuesday, heavy rains will reach the Four Corners region in the western part of the United States and will last until Wednesday.
In October, with the cooling in the Arctic region throughout the United States, temperature fluctuations may occur. However, there will be heat in the inter-tropical zone.
This can lead to rapid temperature changes over short distances.
A good example is Sunday's temperature differences in the middle parts of the United States.
Between Kansas City (Mousore state) and Omaha (Nebraska state) and between St. Louis (Mourosa State) and Des Moines (Iowa state), these differences are almost 10 degrees Celsius.
In the coming days, sustained summer temperatures could rise again.
In much of the central and eastern U.S., a warm beginning of October is expected. Temperatures should fluctuate from 26 to 32 degrees Celsius in the area from the southern part of the Great Plains to part of the Northeast.
On Tuesday in New York, the temperature could reach a value of up to 27 degrees Celsius, which is about 5 degrees above average.
Long-term climatic forecasts indicate a high likelihood of above-average temperatures occurring in the east of the United States in the first half of October.
More than 20 million people have watched Brett Kavanauguh's interrogations
More than 20 million people have watched on six television stations moving Thursday's hearing of Supreme Court Justice candidate Brett Kavanaugh and Christina Blasey Ford, a woman accusing a lawyer of sexual harassing taking place in the 1980s.
Despite an ongoing political deadlock on Friday, commentators had to interrupt regularly broadcast programs to report an abrupt return: an agreement proposed by Senator Jeff Flake, according to which the FBI will conduct a week-long prosecution investigation.
Christine Blasey Ford testified in front of a Senate judiciary that she was 100% sure Kavanaugh groped her while intoxicated and tried to take her clothes off during a high school party.
In his emotional statement Kavanaugh said that he was convinced that such a situation did not happen.
It is possible that Friday’s number of television viewers was even greater than 20.4 millions reported by Nielsen.
The company counted the average number of CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC viewers.
However, for other stations including PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Busian Network, figures have not yet been available.
In addition, Nielsen tends to find it difficult to measure viewerships among people in workplaces.
The number given is similar to the viewership of American football knockout competitions or the awards of the American Film Academy.
According to Nielsen Fox News Channel, whose presenters strongly supported Kavanaugh's nomination, had the highest viewership, amounting to an average of 5.69 million viewers during a full-day hearing.
In second place was ABC with 3.26 million viewers.
CBS had 3.1 million viewers, NBC 2.94 million, MSNBC 2.89 million and CNN 2.52 million.
Interest in the topic remained high after the hearing was completed.
Jeff Flake has become a key figure in Friday's events.
In the early hours of the morning, it was captured by CNN and CBS station cameras after the office of this moderate Republican issued a statement that he had decided to support Kavanaugh's nomination. In the video, he yelled at demonstrators who obstructed him entering the elevator on his way to question the judiciary committee.
Flake with his head down for a few minutes listened to unfavorable opinions. The whole event was broadcast by CNN station.
“I’m standing here in front of you and asking.
Do you think he's telling the nation the truth?
You have the strength in the face of the impotence of so many women," he heard from a demonstrating woman.
Prior to the closure of the elevator door, Jeff Flake said his office had issued an appropriate statement and that he would speak during a committee hearing.
Later, television stations continued to broadcast events in anticipation of a vote over the handover of Kavanaugh's nomination to the entire Senate.
Flake said he would only support the nomination if he knew that the FBI would investigate the accusations next week, which a minority of Democrats insisted.
Flake was persuaded, in part, by conversations with his friend, Senator Chris Coons of the Democratic Party.
Flake made the decision after meeting Chris Coons and several other senators.
Flake's election was important because Republicans wouldn't have the votes needed to approve Brett Kavanaugh's nomination without an investigation.
President Trump has opened FBI proceedings for accusations made against Brett Kavanaugh.
Britain's Prime Minister Theresa May accuses critics of exploiting Brexit for their own benefit
Prime Minister Theresa May accused critics of her plan to leave the European Union for playing Britain's future and undermining the national interest.
Prime Minister Theresa May arrives at the Conservative Party conference in Birmingham (United Kingdom), 29 September 2018.
In another interview, alongside the one depicting Theresa May on the title page, the former foreign secretary in her government, Boris Johnson attacked a plan for a future relationship with the European Union in the aftermath of Brexit, according to which Britain and the EU could collect tariffs on their own behalf, calling it "completely ridiculous."
Wayde Sim's shooting: Police arrested Dyteon Simpson suspected of LSU player's murder
Police have arrested a suspect in connection with the fatal shooting of Wayde Sims, a 20-year-old basketball player featured in Louisiana University (LSU).
According to reports from the Baton Rouge police headquarters, twenty-year-old Dyteon Simpson was detained and placed in custody on suspicion of second-degree murder.
Authorities released footage of a confrontation between Sims and Simpson. Police said Sims had lost his glasses during a brawl.
As reported by CBS-linked WAFB, police officers found these glasses at the scene annd Simpson's DNA traces were detected on them.
Police said after questioning Simpson that he had pleaded guilty to Wayde's shooting.
According to the detainee’s lawyer, the amount of the deposit was set at 350 thousand dollars.
On Friday, the coroner's office in East Baton Rouge issued a preliminary report that said the cause of death was a gunshot wound to the head and neck.
The police headquarters expressed thanks to Louisiana's state police search group, a forensic laboration state police, the Southern University police and nearby residents for their help with the investigation leading to the arrest.
Joe Alleva, LSU's sporting director, thanked local law enforcement agencies for their "diligence and pursuit of justice."
Sims was 20.
Measuring 198 cm, the man grew up in Baton Rouge. His father Wayne Sims also played basketball at LSU.
In his last season, he recorded averages of 5.6 points and 2.6 rebounds per game.
On Friday morning, LSU basketball coach Will Wade said the team was "devastated" and "shaken" by Wayde's death.
“It’s one of those things we’re constantly afraid of,” Will Wade said.
The ashes emitted by the volcano date back to the cities of Mexico
The ashes coming out of the Popocatépetl volcano have reached the southern districts of the Mexican capital.
On Saturday, Mexico's national disaster prevention center advised Mexicans to approach the volcanic due to increased crater activity and 183 cases of gas and ash emissions over 24 hours.
The centre noted numerous rumblings and tremors.
Pictures published on social media show windshield cars covered with a thin layer of ashes in districts of Mexico, such as Xochimilco.
Since the 7.1-degree earthquake that struck Mexico in September 2017, geophysicists have seen an increase in volcanic activity located 72 km southeast of the country's capital.
Also known as Don Goyo has been active since 1994.
Police clash with Catalan separatists ahead of independence referendum anniversary
On Saturday, six people were arrested in Barcelona as a result of clashes of demonstrators supporting the independence of Catalonia with police prevention units. On this day, thousands of people took part in hostile demonstrations commemorating the first announcing referendum on Catalonia's secession.
A group of masked separating supporters pelted police officers with eggs and powder paint. Dark clouds of dust shrouded streets usually full of tourists.
The clashes also took place in the later part of the day. In order to halt fighting, police forces used batons.
For several hours, groups supporting independence chanted “We don’t forget, we don’t forgive”. Opponents of secession shouted “Long live Spain”.
As reported by the local press, fourteen people have received medical assistance for minor injuries sustained during protests.
After a year after the October 1 referendum, the tension did not weaken. Although the authorities in Madrid declared them illegal, it is still a reason to be celebrated for Catalans who wanted secession.
Voters overwhelmingly voted in favour of independence. However, the turnout was low due to a wide-ranging boycott of opponents.
According to Catalan authorities, nearly 1,000 people suffered injuries last year in violent clashes with police trying to prevent polling stations from voting.
On Friday evening, groups of people supporting the independence of the region decided to prevent demonstrations supporting police officers.
The demonstration took place despite this, however, a change of route was forced.
Narcis Termes, a 68-year-old electrician involved in a separatist protest with his wife, said there was no more hope of Catalonia's independence.
“Last year we experienced some of our most beautiful moments in our lives.
My parents were crying with joy that they could vote, but now we're stuck at a no-win point," he said.
Despite a significant but small victory in local elections in December last year, parties supporting the secession of Catalonia are struggling to continue their activities. Many of the most recognized leaders have decided to leave or are awaiting in custody for the trial in relation to the role played in the referendum and the subsequent declaration of independence.
Joan Puig, a forty-two-year-old phone-recording phone protest supporting police, said politicians on both sides of the dispute were fueling the conflict.
“The situation is getting tighter,” he said.
On Saturday, Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders held in custody since the end of last year, announced his desire to run in next year's European Parliament elections.
“Candidation in the European elections is the best way to condemnate the crisis of the democratic values of the Spanish government,” he said.
Londonderry: men arrested after driving car into house
Three men aged 33, 34 and 39 were arrested in the aftermath of hitting a car several times into a house in the city of Londonderry.
The incident happened at Ballynagard Crescent on Thursday at around 19:30 British time (BST).
Commissioner for Investigativing and Investigation Department Bob Blemmings said the damage covered the gate and the building.
At some point towards the car could also be fired from the crossbow.
Menga's goal provides Livingston win over Rangers 1:0
Dolly Menga's first goal for Livingston brought the team victory
Since this season, Livingston has set the Rangers in bewilderment, dealing Steven Gerrard only a second defeat in his 186 matches as club coach from Ibrox Park.
Dolly Menga's goal turned out to be victorious, and Gary Holt's team moved up to second place in the table with Hibernian.
Gerrard's team remains this season without a victory away at the Premiership. Next Sunday, the team will face the Hearts leader, to whom he loses eight points.
Earlier, as early as Thursday, the Rangers will host the Rapid Vienza team as part of the UEFA Europa League.
Livingston is extending a series of victories in his division to six games. Coach Holt hasn't experienced a defeat since replacing Kenny Miler last month.
Livingston have missed a few opportunities against indisposed opponents
The team led by Holt should have taken the lead much earlier, as the team's game brought Rangers a lot of problems.
At first, Scott Robinson broke through the defense and fired a shot into the goal light, but he failed to score a goal. Then Alan Lithgow failed to hit after Craig Halkett's head-to-knock.
The hosts allowed the Rangers to play closer to the goal, knowing they could threaten their rivals during solid fragments of the game.
And that's how the decisive goal fell.
Following a free kick by the Rangers, Livingston found the opportunity. Declan Gallagher and Robinson collectively created the situation of Menga, which scored a goal from the middle of the penalty area.
At this stage, Rangers had more possessions, but they couldn't push through the hosts' defence and didn't cause any major problems for goalkeeper Liam Kelly.
This scheme was repeated in the second half. Alfredo Morelos fired one shot on Kelly’s goal.
Scott Pittman's rehearsal was defended by Rangers goalkeeper Allan McGregor, and Lithgow failed to hit the goal light from another permanent snippet ordered in favour of Livingston.
Rangers continued attacks in Livingston’s penalty area, but they couldn’t take advantage of the situation. They demanded two penalty shootouts after Halkett’s intervention against Glenn Middleton’s replacement and one hand play. In both cases, however, the players’ pretensions were rejected.
“Phenomenal” performance of Livingston – analysis
Alasdair Lamont from BBC Scotland at Almondvale Park
Wonderful performance and result for Livingston.
In his opinion, they played great, once again going beyond expectations.
Their style and line-up have hardly changed since they returned to the extraclass. However, Holt should be appreciated for motivating the team to play.
This match had many heroes.
Captain Craig Halkett played a huge role, directing a well-organized defence, while Menga made Connor Goldson and Joe Worrall have to be very attentive throughout the match.
Rangers lacked the will to fight.
Although there are great moments under Gerrard’s leadership, they didn’t maintain their standards in this match.
In their game, something seemed to be missing: only once managed to break through the hosts’ defense. It should be a serious warning to the Rangers, who found themselves in the middle of the table.
Mixed responses to Erdogan's visit to Cologne
During Saturday's breakfast in Berlin (29 September), the leaders of Turkey and Germany were accompanied by smiles and beautiful weather.
It was the last day of President Erdogan’s controversial visit to Germany. Visits were aimed at improving relations between NATO allies.
Disputes have led to differences in issues such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey’s accession to the European Union.
After the meeting, Erdogan went to Cologne, where he opened a new huge mosque.
In this city lives the largest number of Turks outside their homeland.
For security reasons, a 25-thousand-year-old crowd gathered in front of the temple were blocked, however. Supporters of the president, however, gathered in the area to see their leader.
Hundreds of Erdogan's opponents - including many Kurds - have also decided to express their opinion, condemning Turkey's leader's policies and the German government's decision to adopt a politician in the country.
Opposing protests, they reflect the divisions raised by the visit of a politician hailed as a hero by some Turks living in Germany and considered an autocrat by others.
Deptford road traffic accident: Cyclist died as a result in car collision
A cyclist died after a collision with a car in London.
The accident occurred near a crowded intersection of Bestwood Street and Evelyn Street in Deptford, the city's south-eastern district, at about 10:15 a.m
After the incident, the driver stopped the vehicle, and paramedics arrived at the scene. Despite the intervention, the man died at the scene of the accident.
A few months ago on Childers Street (about one and a half kilometers away from the scene of Saturday’s accident) there was another fatal accident involving a cyclist.
Metropolitan police have reported that officers are working to identify the dead man and inform family members about the incident.
Roads were closed and bus detours were organized, and drivers were instructed to avoid the nearby area.
Long Lartin's corrective facility: Six officers sustained riot injuries
As reported by the authorities of the facility, six officers suffered injuries as a result of riots in a men's correctional facility with a higher level of safety.
Riots at HMP Long Lartin's correctional facility in Worcestershire began on Sunday around 9:30 a.m. British time (BST) and are still ongoing.
For riots involving eight prisoners in one of the wings of the plant, a specialized unit "Tornado" was called.
Officers staying at the scene suffered minor injuries to their faces.
A spokesperson of the plant said: "Specially trained personnel have been involved at HMP Long Lartin's correctional facility.
Six officers suffered minor injuries.
“We do not tolerate violence in our facilities. We are convinced that those responsible will be handed over to police and can spend more time in prison.”
There are more than 500 inmates, including some of the country's most dangerous criminals.
According to reports in June, the director of the facility had to go to the hospital after he was attacked by one of the prisoners.
In October last year, prevention units were called to the plant due to threatening riots, under which employees were attacked with bullets to play billiards.
Sudden flooding caused by Hurricane Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City (possibly a beneficial effect on drought areas)
It is rare for tropical depression to reach the state of Arizona. This is likely to happen early next week. Remains of Hurricane Rosa are moving through the desert areas of the Southern West, posinging a threat of sudden flooding.
The National Weather Service has already issued a warning about the possibility of emergencious flooding on Monday and Tuesday in western Arizona, southern and eastern Nevada, south-eastern California and Utah, including cities such as Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
According to the forecasts on Tuesday, Rosa will pass directly over Phoenix. On Monday afternoon, the rainfall will begin.
“Since 1950, just ten tropical cyclones have maintained a state of storm or tropical depression at a distance of 320 km from Phoenix.
Hurricane Katrina (in 1967) approached a distance of 65 km from the Arizona state border.”
The latest National Hurricane Center models forecast precipitations ranging in height from 5 to 10 cm. In some areas of Mogollon Rim in Arizona, they can reach heights of up to 15 cm.
In the other desert areas of the Southern West – including the central Rocky Mountains and the Great Basin – the precipitation height is probably between 2.5 and 5 cm, and in some areas up to 10 cm.
Persons from areas at risk of sudden flood precipitation accompanying Hurricane Rosa may provide relief, as the region has been struggling with drought for some time.
Although floods pose a serious threat, some precipitation will improve the situation in the South West, where there is a state of drought.
According to weather.com, U.S. Drought Monitor estimates that nearly 40% of the Arizona area face severe droughts belonging to the second highest categories.
Hurricane Rosa arrives first at the California Peninsula in Mexico.
On Sunday morning, Rosa continued to move with hurricane force, reaching speeds of up to 137 km/h. It is currently located 620 km south of Punta Eugenia in Mexico and heading north at 19 km/h.
Hurricane encounters the cooler waters of the Pacific Ocean, resulting in its waning.
According to forecast, it will hit Mexico with the force of a tropical storm on Monday afternoon or evening.
Intense rainfall in some parts of Mexico could pose a major flood risk.
Weather.com reported that "in the area from the California Peninsula to the north-western part of the state of Sonora, precipitation with a height of 7 to 15 cm is predicted, although they can reach 25 cm."
Hurricane Rosa will then pass through Mexico towards the north in the form of a tropical storm. On Tuesday, in the early hours of the morning, it will reach the Arizona state's border as a tropical depression. By Tuesday evening, it will travel to the southern part of the state of Utah.
“The most serious threat posed by Hurricane Rosa or its remnants are very heavy rainfall on the California Peninsula, in the north-west of Sonora, and in the southwestern desert areas of the United States,” the National Hurricane Center reported.
According to the forecasts, rains will cause life-threatening floods and the movement of pollution in the desert and mud avalanches in mountainous terrain.
Midsomer Norton attack: Four people arrested in attempted murder
Three teenagers and a twenty-year-old man were arrested on suspicion of the attempted murder of a sixteen-year-old found with stab wounds in Somerset.
The boy was found in Excelsior Terrace, in Midsomer Norton, on Saturday, around 4:00 a.m. British time (BST).
The victim was taken to hospital and her condition remains "stable."
Police from Avon and Somerset reported that one seventeen-year-old, two eighteen-years-old and a twenty-year-old man, were arrested in the Radstock area at night.
Officers ask to be contacted by all persons with recordings of the event.
Trump stated that Brett Kavanaugh "has experienced the meanness and angery" of the Democratic Party
"The vote cast for Judge Kavanaugh is a vote against the ruthless and outrageous conduct of the Democratic Party," Trump said at a rally in Wheeling, West Virginia.
Trump claimed Kavanaugh had "explored the meanness and wrath" of the Democratic Party during the nomination process.
On Thursday, Brett Kavanaugh testified before the congress. He firmly and emotionally denied Christina Blasey Ford’s accusations. The woman claims that a few decades ago, when they were both teenagers, the man committed sexual assault against her.
Ford also testified during a hearing in the case of her allegations.
On Saturday, the president said Kavanaugh's "U.S. people could convince themselves of their brilliance, class and courage."
“The voice cast for Judge Kavanaugh is the voice of one of the most outstanding lawyers of our time, an expert on the law of great merit to the community,” he said.
The president did not explicitly address Brett Kavanaugh's nomination, discussing the importance of Republican turnout in the half-election presidency.
“We are five weeks away from the most important choices in our lives.
“I don’t run in this election, but I remain very active,” he said.
“I’m constantly fighting to defend our great candidates.”
Trump says Democrats are pursuing a policy of "resisting and obstrucing activity."
As one of the assistants of an important Republican politician told CNN, the first key Senate vote on Kavanaugh's nomination is expected by Friday at the latest.
Hundreds of earthquake and tsunami death toll in Indonesia
Authorities said at least 384 people died on Saturday—many of whom were hijacked by high waves crashing into beaches—in the aftermath of a strong earthquake and tsunami that hit Indonesia’s Sulawesi island.
On Friday, hundreds of people gathered on the beach to take part in a festival held in the city. At dusk, waves reaching up to 6 meters high, kidnapping many people and destroying everything on their way.
Tsunami was the result of an earthquake of 7.5 degrees.
"When there was a threat yesterday, those on the beach didn't escape immediately and fell victim to a tsunami," Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, a spokesperson for the Indonesian disaster agency BNPD, told a conference in Jakarta.
“Tsunami attacked everything on its way, hijacking cars, logs and houses,” Nugroho reported. He then added that before reaching the shoreline, the element was moving at sea at 800 km/h.
As he said, some rescued themselves by climbing trees and managed to survive.
Approximately 16,700 people were evacuated to 24 centres in Palu.
Aerial photographs published by the disaster agency show a number of destroyed homes and shops, damaged and collapsed bridges and a mosque surrounded by water.
On Saturday, the coastal city was hit by aftershocks.
There have been a number of earthquakes in the area inhabited by 2.4 million people.
According to a statement from the Indonesian Technology Assessment and Use Agency (BPPT), the energy triggered by an extensive Friday earthquake was about two hundred times the energy of an atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima during World War II.
The statement stated that the geographical location of the city, which is located at the end of a long, narrow bay, may have compounded the effects of the tsunami.
Sutopo Purwo Nugroho described the damage as "significant" and said hundreds of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels had collapsed.
He then added that the bodies of parts of the victims were found under collapsed buildings, 540 people were injured and 29 people were presumed missing.
Nugroho said the fatalities and damage could be greater along the shoreline 300 km north of Palu, in an area called Donggala, which was closer to the epicentre of the quake.
According to Nugroho, communications "have been completely paralyzed," so there are no reports from Donggala province.
“More than 300 thousand people live there,” the Red Cross said in a statement. It was also added that the organization’s staff and volunteers are going to places affected by defeat.
"We can already talk about the tragedy, although the situation may deteriorate significantly," the statement said.
On Saturday, the agency faced widespread criticism due to a lack of information about a tsunami strike in Palu. Authorities, however, said the waves came shortly after the warning was issued.
An amateur footage shared on social media shows a man screaming from the building, trying to warn about an incoming tsunami of people on the street.
Within minutes, a wall of water crashed into the shore, hijacking buildings and cars.
Reuters was unable to immediately confirm the authenticity of the recording.
The earthquake and tsunami caused significant shortages in the supply of electricity, preventing communication within Pal. This, in turn, made it difficult for the authorities to coordinate rescue efforts.
According to the authorities, the military has started sending transport planes with help from Jakarta and other cities, but evacuees still badly need food and other basic essentials.
Located in the city, the airport was opened exclusively for transports with humanitarian assistance. The airport will remain closed until October.
On Sunday, President Joko Widodo is scheduled to visit centers for people evacuated in Palu.
More than 800 tsunami deaths in Indonesia.
Situation is very bad.
As Doseba T. Sinay, the national director of World Vision, reported, the personnel of this organization arrived securely from Donggala province to the city of Palu and found shelter in tarp tents placed in the courtyard of the office. Along the way, the workers passed many ravaged areas.
“They told me they’d saw a lot of destroyed houses,” he said.
Situation is very bad.
Although some groups are slowly starting to provide assistance, there have been voices of criticism that employees of foreign humanitarian organizations with extensive experience are prevented from traveling to Palu.
According to Indonesian law, receiving funding, resources and personnel from abroad can only be started after announcing the site of the disaster with an area of national disaster.
That hasn't been done yet.
"Officially, we are still dealing with a regional disaster," a spokesman for the Indonesian Red Cross said.
“Once the government recognizes that there has been a national defeat, we will be able to start accepting international aid, but this has not happened yet.”
Two days after Friday's Palu earthquake and tsunami, friends and families of missing people still hold the hope that their loved ones will somehow find themselves in the midst of destruction.
On Saturday, a little boy was pulled from the canals.
On Sunday, paramedics released the woman, which spent two days under the rubble next to her mother's body.
Gendon Subandono, coach of the Indonesian paragliding team, trained two missing paragliders taking part in this month's Asian Olympics.
As Petra Mandaga said, other people trapped in the Roa Roa hotel were his pupils.
“As an experienced paraglider, I struggle with my own emotional burden,” he said.
Gendon Subandono recounted how, within a few hours of the paraglider's appearance among the paraglider community, information about the collapsion of the Roa Roa Hotel was desperately sending messages via WhatsApp messenger to athletes taking part in the festival on the beach.
His messages were marked with only one gray hook instead of a pair of hooks in blue.
“It seems to me that this means that the messages have not been delivered,” he said.
Thieves stolen USD 26 750 while replenishing cash at an ATM at Newport on the Levee shopping mall
According to Newport's police headquarters on Friday morning, thieves stole USD 26 750 to a Brink employee who was replenising cash at an ATM at Newport on the Leve shopping mall.
"The driver of the car emptied the ATM in the entertainment complex and was preparing to replenish the cash," Detective Dennis McCarthy said in a statement.
Another man "ran up from behind to a Brink employee" and stole a bag with money for an ATM.
According to the communiqué, witnesses saw several suspects running away from the scene. Police, however, did not say how many people were involved in the theft.
All persons with information on the identity of thieves are asked to contact the Newport police station at 859-292-3680.
Kanye West: rapper changes name to Ye
Rapper Kanye West decided to change his name to Ye.
Announcing his intention on Twitter on Saturday, he wrote about himself: "A person formerly known as Kanye West."
The forty-one-year-old musician has been using the pseudonym Ye for some time. It is also the title of his eighth album released in June.
The change occurred prior to a visit to Saturday Night Live. The artist is expected to present his new Yandhi album during the performance.
He will replace the singer Ariana Grande, which, according to the creators, resigned “for emotional reasons.”
West has previously stated that the word “Ye” is not only an abbreviation of its current artistic nickname, but has a religious meaning.
“It seems to me that ‘ye’ is the most commonly used word in the Bible. In Scripture, it means ‘you’,” West said in an earlier period of the year, discussing the title of an album with a radio presenter nicknamed Big Boy.
“So I’m you, I’m us, it’s about us.
First, I was Kanye, which means the only person. Now I’m Ye – a reflection of all our good, bad and confusing qualities.
The album shows who we are.”
West was among the other famous rappers who decided to change their name.
Sean Combs, known as Puff Daddy, P. Diddy and Diddy, announced this year that he wants to be called Love and Brother Love.
JAY-Z, a former collaborator of West, also wrote down his pseudonym in various ways, with a connector, without a connector and in capital letters.
Mexican President Andrés Manuel López Obrador pledges not to use troops against civilians
On the eve of the 50th anniversary of the bloody suppression of student demonstrations, Mexican President-elect Andrés Manuel López Obrador (AMLO) pledged never to use the military against civilians.
On Saturday in the square of the Three Cultures López Obrador promised "never to use the military to repress the Mexican people."
On October 2, 1968, soldiers opened fire on the square towards a peaceful demonstration, killing up to 300 people. The incident occurred at a time when left-wing student movements were beginning to develop throughout Latin America.
López Obrador has vowed to support young Mexican residents by providing monthly financial assistance to learners as well as creating more free public colleges.
He said unemployment and a lack of educational opportunities pushes the young towards criminal groups.
The United States should double funding for artificial intelligence research
According to Kai-Fu Lee, an investor and expert in artificial intelligence working for Google, Microsoft and Apple, as China’s activity grows in this area, the United States should reduce spending on AI research.
The remarks are related to announcements on artificial intelligence published by various U.S. government agencies, despite the fact that the U.S. does not have an official AI strategy.
Meanwhile, last year China unveiled its plan, according to which it wants to become the most innovative country in the field of artificial intelligence by 2030.
“To begin with, we need to redouble the AI research budget: all other countries are far behind the United States, and we are trying to make another breakthrough in this area,” Kai-Fu Lee said.
In an interview this week, CNBC also said doubling funds could double the chances of making another artificial intelligence breakthrough in the United States.
Lee, whose book “AI Superpowers: China, Silicon World Order) was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcoud, is the president of Sinovation Ventures. The company has invested in one of the most notable companies dealing with artificial intelligence in China.
In the 1980s, Kai-Fu Lee worked at Carnegie Mellon University to create an AI system that defeated America’s best Othello player at the time. He then held a managerial position at Microsoft Research and was the president of Google’s Chinese division.
Lee acknowledged the success of earlier technological initiatives of the U.S. government, e.g
As Kai-Fu Lee said, scientists in the United States often have to try very hard to receive government subsidies.
“It’s not China that’s taking away our top academia, it’s the corporations.”
For several years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have been hiring distinguished scientists to work on artificial intelligence.
According to Lee, changes in immigration policy could also help the United States develop AI.
“I believe that those dealing with artificial intelligence who have obtained a doctoral degree should automatically admit a green card,” he said.
In July 2017, the Chinese State Council published a plan to create next-generation artificial intelligence.
As Lee pointed out, China’s National Natural Science Foundation of China provides funding to members of academic institutions in a similar way to the U.S. National Science Foundation and other government organizations granting funds to U.S. researchers. The quality of academic activity in China, however, is lower.
In an earlier period of this year, the U.S. Department of Defense established a Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, which will involve partners from the world of industry and science. The White House, on the other hand, announced the creation of a special committee on Artificial Intelligence.
DARPA also announced this month the allocation of US$2 billion to an initiative called AI Next.
The National Science Foundation currently invests more than US$100 billion a year in AI research.
At the same time, the U.S. initiative to create a national AI security commission hasn't moved forward for months.
The Macedonians are voting in the referendum to change the name of their state
On Sunday, the Macedonians voted in a referendum on changing the name of their country to the Republic of North Macedonia. This movement would allow to avert a decades-long dispute with Greece, which is blocking the country's efforts to join the European Union and NATO.
Greece, where Macedonia’s historical land is located, maintains that the name of its northern neighbor constitutes a claim to its territory. As a result, it vetoed the country’s accession to the EU and NATO.
In June, the governments of both states reached an agreement on naming. The nationalists, however, say the change would undermine the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority living in Macedonia.
President �дorge Ivanov said he would not take part in the vote. A campaign encouraging a boycott raised doubts as to whether the turnout would reach the minimum level of 50% required to declare the referendum binding.
The referendum asked the following question: "Do you support joining NATO and the EU in the aftermath of accepting an agreement with Greece."
Proponents of changing the name, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, say that it is worth paying the price for the possibility of joining Macedonia – one of the countries created after the fall of Yugoslavia – to such organizations as the European Union or NATO.
“I voted for the future of my country so that young people of Macedonia can live free under the umbrella of the European Union. Membership in the EU will provide us all with greater security,” said 79-year-old Oliwera Georgijewska from Skopje.
Although the referendum is not legally binding, a sufficient number of MPs have pledged to respect the results of the vote.
Changing the name requires the support of two-third members of parliament.
The state's electoral commission said no unusual events had been reported until 13 p.m.
However, the turnout at this hour was only 16% compared to 34% in the last parliamentary elections of 2016. At that time, 66% of eligible people cast their vote.
“I voted because of my children. Our place is in Europe,” said 62-year-old Ǵosze Tanewski, voter from Skopje.
On September 30, 2018, in the city of Strumica in Macedonia, the country's prime minister Zoran Zaew, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko cast their vote in a referendum on changing the name of the country to open the way to membership of NATO and the European Union.
In front of the parliament building in Skopje, 54-year-old Vladimir Cavardarkov prepared a small scene and set up chairs in front of tents unleashed by people boycotting the referendum.
“We support membership of NATO and the EU, but we want to join these organisations with their heads raised, not the back door.”
“We are a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they don’t want us as Macedonia, we can turn to other countries such as China or Russia and become part of Eurasian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaew argues that NATO membership will bring Macedonia's needed investment, where the unemployment rate is more than 20%.
“I believe that the vast majority will vote in favor of changing the name in the referendum, because 80% of the citizens support the European Union and NATO,” Zaew said.
He stated that voting for a "yes" constitutes a "confirmation of our future."
In a poll published last Monday by the Macedonian public research institute, between 30 and 43% of voters declared their desire to participate in the referendum. This is less than the required turnout.
According to another poll conducted by Macedonia's Telma TV in Sunday's referendum, 57% of respondents plan to vote.
70% of those surveyed said they would support changing their names.
In order for the vote to be successful, turnout must amount to 50% + 1 vote.
Defeat in the referendum would be the first major blow to pro-Western government's policies since taking power in May last year.
Sergio Aguero of Manchester City beat Brighton's entire defender and scores goal
Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling have sent home Brighton's defence in Saturday's meeting ended with a score of 2:0 for Manchester City. The match was played at Etihad Stadium in Manchester (England).
Goal scored by Aguera in the 65th minute looked childily easy.
The action began by passing in the middle of the pitch to an Argentine striker.
The footballer surpassed Brighton's three defenders.
Aguero was then among Brighton's four players.
He played one of them first, and then he surpassed the others and found himself on the brink of Brighton's penalty area.
That's when he played the ball to the left, where Sterling was waiting for it.
The English striker immediately handed the ball back to Aguera in the penalty area, who beat Brighton goalkeeper Mathew Ryan with a shot to the right side of the goal.
"Aguero is facing a foot injury," Manchester City coach Pep Guardiola told reporters.
“We talked about the possibility of playing 55, 60 minutes.
And that's exactly what happened.
We were lucky he scored in that time.”
However, it was Sterling who ensured blue's first lead in the Premier League.
The goal in question came in the 29th minute.
In this action, Aguero received the ball deeply on Brighton's half.
He sent a wonderful pass along the left side of the pitch to Leroy Sane.
Sane made several moves, allowing Sterling to move to the far post.
The Manchester City striker headed the ball into the net just before it fell out on the cars.
Manchester City will face Hoffenheim in the UEFA Champions League group stage on Tuesday at 12:55 p.m. The meeting will take place at Rhein-Neckar-Arena Stadium in Sinsheim (Germany).
Scherzer wants to spoil Rockies' chances
After losing his chances of taking part in the play-off stage by the Washington Nationals, Scherzer didn't have to feature in the starting line-up.
