Rockies can at least count on a wild card if they win Friday’s meeting against the Nationals with a score of 5:2. However, they still want to get their first division title.
“Despite the fact that we’re playing ‘for nothing’, at least we’ll get out on the field, knowing that the atmosphere in Denver and the opponents’ performance will be at the highest level as in the rest of the season.
“Why wouldn’t I want to participate in this meeting?”
The Nationals have not yet announced the starting pitcher for Sunday's meeting. However, the team is leaning towards putting Scherzer in the starting line-up.
It would have been Scherzer's thirty-fourth appearance in the starting line-up line-up. He took part in Thursday's throwing practice and is hoping to perform on Sunday.
The right-handed thrower of the team from Washington recorded results of 18-7 and ERA 2.53. He eliminated the batters 300 times and scored 220 and 2/3 rounds per season.
Trump's rally in West Virginia
Trump did not explicitly refer to Brett Kavanaugh's nomination as a Supreme Court justice, discussing the importance of Republican turnout in the half-premises election.
“In November, we put all the achievements at stake so far.
One of the most important choices in our lives is five weeks away.
Although I don’t run, I remain very active. I’m constantly defending our great candidates,” he said.
He then continued, “We’re dealing with a terrible group of radical Democrats. We see their actions with our own eyes.”
They are determined to take power by all possible means. They are vile and malicious.
They don’t care who they’re going to hurt and who they have to hurt in order to gain power and control. Embracing power and control is their purpose. However, we won’t give it back to them.”
As he said, the Democrats are pursuing a mission to "resist and obstruct business."
"This has been very well seen in the last four days," he said, calling the Democrates "evil, vicious, unpleasant and untrue."
The president referred to the Senate judiciary's committee, citing U.S.-named-named U.S. Democratic Senator Dianne Feinstein, meeting a loud boom from the crowd.
“Do you remember her answer?
Are you responsible for a document leak?
“Oh, am I listening?
No... I mean... no». Her bodily speech was terrible. It was the worst body speech I’ve ever seen.”
Labour is no longer a group of people with different views.
The party is intolerant to those expressing their own opinions
When Momentum activists in my party voted to give me a reprimand, I wasn't surprised.
In the end, I'm a member of a group of Labour MPs who have recently been told that they're not welcome, and all because of their own opinions.
My parliamentary colleague Joan Ryan faced similar treatment because she objected to anti-Semitism.
In my case, the rebuke request was due to a disagreement between me and Jeremy Corbyn.
The differences concerned the importance of responsible economic policies, national security and Europe. Ironically, Jeremy Corbyn opposed the position of previous leaders on these issues.
In Friday's announcement of a meeting by Labour MPs from the Nottingham East constituency, it was said: "we want the meetings to be open and productive."
For eight years, I have served as a Labour MP for most of Friday’s meetings of the General Committee, this is what it looked like.
Unfortunately, many meetings are going differently now. It’s been long forgotten about the promise of “warmer and gentler” politics, if you’ve ever remembered it.
It is increasingly obvious that there are no different views being tolerated in the Labour Party. Every opinion is assessed in terms of compliance with the leadership of the party.
It all started not long after Jeremy Corbyn took the position of chairman. Colleagues, for whom it seemed to me that we had similar views, began to expect me to change direction and take positions with which I would have previously disagreed. They concerned issues such as national security or the EU single market.
After every public appearance – regardless of the content – I encounter on social media attacks calling for nominations to be stripped, to condemn centrism or to leave the Labour Party.
It's not just my experience.
I am more fortunate than some of my colleagues, because the remarks directed at my address are rather political in nature.
I admire the professionalism and determination of colleagues who experience treatment in a sexist or racistic way on a daily basis, but do not avoid further action.
One of the most disappointing aspects of the current policy is the transition to the agenda with offending others.
Last week Jeremy Corbyn stated that Labour should support a culture of tolerance.
In fact, we are no longer a group of people with different views. With every request for a “vote of no confidence” and any change in the rules for the choice of the party’s worldview, it narrows.
Over the past two years, I’ve been repeatedly suggesting that I don’t lean out and keep some issues silent. Back then, everything “would be fine.”
But that’s not whay I dealt with politics.
Since joining the Labour Party 32 years ago as a pupil as a result of negligence of Margaret Thatcher’s government, which led to the break-up of my school classroom, I try to take care of the better quality of public services for those in need – both as a local councillor and a minister.
I've never hid my convictions, even during my last election campaign.
Everyone in Nottingham East district knew my position and they knew about the differences of opinion with the party's authorities.
I would like to say to those who supported Friday’s proposal that I cannot understand the desire to waste time and energy on the issue of my loyalty to Labour in the face of an impending Brexit that will harm households, businesses and public services.
The most important message, however, is addressed not to Nottingham Momentum, but to my constituents (both members of the Labour Party and the others): I am proud that I can serve you. I promise that no threats to undo the nominations or ad hoc political goals will prevent me from acting in accordance with my convictions and in the best interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie is an MP from the Nottingham East
Ayr vs. Melrose 38:17 – Undefeated Ayr rises again
Two late touchdowns may have somehow distorted the final result. However, there is no doubt that Ayr's team deserved victory in this extremely interesting Scottish extra-league match.
The team came first in the table and remains the only unbeaten team.
At the end, it's a standout defense and a better take-up of the odds that led the hosts' team to victory. Coach Peter Murchie may have been happy with his pupils.
"Matches so far have been a challenge for us, but we're still unbeaten, so I'm happy," he said.
