“The sea belongs to us”: deprived of access to the sea, Bolivia hopes that the judgment will reopen its way to the Pacific
Sailors are patrolling the vicinity of the naval base in La Paz.
From public buildings there are blue flags.
In naval ports from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon there is the following motto: "The sea is ours by law.
Getting it back is our duty.”
In not having access to the sea of Bolivia, the memory of the shoreline lost to Chile in a bloody conflict over resources from the 19th century is still alive. Quitely like dreams of re-sailing in the Pacific Ocean.
These hopes are perhaps the greatest in several decades. Bolivia is awaiting the judgment of the International Court of Justice. It is to be handed down on October 1 after a five-year trial.
"Bolivia's actions are gaining momentum. Residents are unanimous, optimistic and are counting on a favorable verdict," Bolivian diplomat Roberto Calzadilla said.
Many Bolivians across the country will jointly watch the meeting of the International Court of Justice. They hope that the Hague-based organization will recognize Bolivia’s claim and, after decades of fruitless talks, Chile will have to enter into negotiations to grant the state independent access to the sea.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic president, who will face a controversial re-election battle next year, also puts hope in Monday's ruling.
"We're very close to returning to the Pacific," he vowed at the end of August.
However, some analysts say that the chances of a favorable ruling for Bolivia are small, and that even a favorable decision would not change much.
The Hague-based United Nations have no authority to confess Chile to Bolivia. It also stipulated that it will not affect the findings of possible talks.
As Paz Zárate, a Chilean specialist in international law said, the decision of the International Court of Justice will be issued six months after the presentation of the final arguments. Such a short time shows that the matter “was not complicated.”
Unluckily, Bolivia's past four years have been able to distance the country from its target.
“The issue of access to the sea has been appropriated by the current Bolivian administration,” said Paz Zárate.
Morales' relentless rhetoric deprived the Chileans of any remnants of goodwill, she suggested.
Bolivia and Chile will resume talks at some point, but it will be very difficult in the face of the situation.
There has been no exchange of ambassadors for both countries since 1962.
Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, a former president and Bolivia's representative in The Hague, disagreed with the opinion that the court's decision-making process was extremely swift.
Monday will bring Bolivia a "unique opportunity to start a new era in terms of relations with Chile" and an opportunity to "end the 139-year-old period of disputes involving mutual benefits," he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales, still one of Latin America’s most popular presidents, used the topic of access to the sea as part of a political game.
"Bolivia will never give up the right of access to the Pacific Ocean," he added.
“Judgment is an excellent opportunity to believe that we need to deal with the past.”
North Korean authorities say nuclear disarmament will not happen until they can trust the United States.
Norther Korea's foreign minister, Ri Yong Ho, states that his nation will never get rid of nuclear weapons as long as it can trust Washington.
Ri spoke at the U.N. General Assembly on Saturday.
He urged the United States to keep its promises made during the Singapore summit between the leaders of the two countries.
His comments came at a time when U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears to be on the brink of resuming suspended nuclear diplomacy more than three months after a meeting in Singapore with North Korean leader Kim Jong Une.
Ri argues that it is a “gloom” that the failure to abolish sanctions and the U.S. opposition to the declaration ending the Korean war has ever brought North Korea to its knees.
Washington refuses to agree to sign the declaration without Pyongyang first taking concrete disarmament steps.
Both Kim Jong Un and U.S. President Donald Trump want another meeting.
However, there is widespread skepticism that Pyongyang is serious about decommissioning the arsenal, which is seen in North Korea as the only means to guarantee safety.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month in preparation for Kim-Trump's second summit.
Parish fashion shows herald the appearance of the latest line of great headgear in clothing stores.
If you want to enlarge your collection of hats or completely cover yourself from the sun, then we have good information for you.
Fashion designers Valentino and Thom Browne have unveiled a range of crazy, oversized headgear included in their 2019 summer collection that dazzled in style during Paris Fashion Week.
Extremely impractical hats towered on Instagram this summer. These designers decided to present their stunning creations on the catwalk.
Valentino's distinguished work was a giant beige hat decorated with a wide, feathered roundabout flooding the models' heads.
Other oversized accessories included watermelons decorated with jewels, a wizard’s hat, and even a pineapple, but they were not designed to warm the head.
Thom Browne also showed off a number of peculiar masks — just in time for Halloween.
Many colorful masks had their lips sewn up and were more reminiscent of Hannibal Lecter's style than haute couture.
One creation resembled diving equipment with snorkel and glasses, and another looked like molten ice cream.
And if you don't want to break up with great fashion, you're lucky.
Observers of styles predict that huge hats can go to clothing stores.
Unsized hats follow “La Bomba”, a straw hat with a 61 cm wide roundabout, which was already worn by everyone – from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The iconic brand behind this extremely impractical hat, which spilled through social media, presented another great creation on the catwalk – a straw beach bag almost as large as the model wearing it in a swimsuit.
The burnt-off orange raffia bag, finished with raffia tassels and topped with a white leather handle, stood out in the Jacquemus La Riviera collection for the 2019 summer season during Paris Fashion Week.
Luke Armitage, celebrity stylist FEMAIL: “I expect large hats and beach bags to appear in stores the next summer – due to the fact that the designer managed to make such a big impact, it would be difficult not to take advantage of the demand for oversized accessories.”
John Edward: language skills necessary for citizens of the world
Independent schools in Scotland are constantly enjoying their scientific achievements – in 2018 they were able to boast further outstanding exam results, supported by individual and collective successes in sports, arts, music and other social endeavors.
Represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), schools attended by more than 30,000 pupils across Scotland are aiming to provide the best level of service provided to pupils and parents.
Independent schools aim to prepare students for further and higher education, their chosen career path and to indicate their role as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector with the opportunity to develop and implement a curriculum tailored to the needs of the school, we can see that modern languages are still popular and desirable subjects to choose from in schools.
Nelson Mandela once said, “If you talk to another man in a language he understands, then it goes into his head.
If you speak to him in his own language, it goes to his heart.”
This is a clear reminder that we can't rely only on English when we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
The results of the last exam this year show that languages rank first in the league rankings, with the highest pass rates occurring for independent schools.
A total of 68 percent of students who studied foreign languages received a higher A grade.
Data collected from 74 SCIS member schools found that 72 per cent of pupils scored A in Mandarin, and 72 per cent of German learners, 69 per cent of French learners and 63 per cent of Spanish learners also earned an A rating.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as the necessary skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Currently, language learning, as electives, is conducted in the same scope as learning STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) as part of curriculars in independent schools and elsewhere.
A 2014 study conducted by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills found that employers had a 17 percent lack of candidate language skills as a reason for difficulties in staffing vacancies.
That is why language skills are increasingly becoming a necessity to prepare young people to start their professional careers.
These skills are essential in a globalised world with a growing number of jobs requiring knowledge of languages,
A lifelong skill, such as learning a second language, will provide a huge advantage in the future – regardless of your chosen path of professional development.
The ability to communicate directly with people from other countries automatically increases the chances of a multilingual person in relation to competition.
According to a 2013 YouGov poll attended by more than 4,000 British adults, 75% of them were unable to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, and the French language was the only language whose percentage of speakers was double-digit, i.e. 15%.
That’s why investing in teaching foreign languages is so important for today’s children.
Knowing several languages, in particular the languages of developing economies, will provide children with a better chance of finding suitable employment.
In Scotland, each school will be different in terms of the languages taught.
Many schools will focus on more classic modern languages, while others will teach languages that are most important to the UK in the perspective of 2020, such as Mandarin or japanese.
Regardless of the interests of the child, there will always be many languages to choose from in independent schools, which will be taught by specialized teaching staff in this area.
Scotch independent schools are committed to creating a teaching environment that will prepare children and arm them with the skills necessary to succeed – regardless of what the future holds for them.
Currently, there is no denying that, in the international business environment, languages are still crucial for the future of the country, and so this must be reflected in education.
Modern languages should be treated as “international communication skills.”
Independent schools will continue to offer Scottish youth a great selection, diversity and level.
That's just the way it belongs.
John Edward, President of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
LeBron's debut in the Lakers on Sunday in San Diego.
Fans wanting to see LeBron James' debut at the Los Angeles Lakers have finally arrived.
Luke Walton, the Lakers coach, announced that LeBron James will play in Sunday's pre-season game against the Denver Nuggets team in San Diego.
