Trump's election rally in West Virginia, YouTube channel
President Donadl Trump is starting a series of campaign rallies this evening in Wheeling, West Virginia.
It's the first Trump-planned campaign rallies to be held next week, along with stopovers at friendly venues such as Tennessee and Mississippi.
Along with a vote confirming the suspension of his election to fill a vacant position in the Supreme Court, Trump seeks to build support in the forthcominging election at the halfway point of government, as Republicans may face a loss of control over Congress when the votes are cast in November.
At what time will Trump’s campaign rally take place in West Virginia this evening, and where will you be able to watch it online?
Trump's campaign rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, will take place today at 19.00 p.m., i.e. Saturday, September 29, 2018.
Trump's campaign rally in West Virginia can be followed live online on YouTube.
Trump is likely to speak out about this week's hearings of Supreme Court nominated candidate Brett Kavanaugh, which took place in a tense atmosphere over allegations of sexual misbehavior, as well as an anticipated Senate vote to suspend selection for a period of up to one week, in terming the FBI's investigation.
However, the main goal of this series of campaign rallies is to provide support to Republicans facing difficult November elections.
That's so, President Trump's five-voltage campaign next week aims "to mobilize volunteers and supporters to assist Republicans in protecting and expanding the majority in the Senate and the House of Representatives."
“Congressional scrutiny is such an important point in the president’s timetable that he will visit as many states as he approaches the hottest season of the campaign,” Trump said.
Today’s election rally at the Wesbanco Arena in Wheeling can attract supporters from “Ohio and Pennsylvania, as well as the attention of the media from Pittsburgh” — West Virginia Metro News.
On Saturday, Trump will visit Western Virginia — the state where he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016 — for the second time in the last month.
Trump is trying to help West Virginia's nominee for the Senate, Republican Patrick Morrisey losing in the polls.
“That’s not a good sign for Morrisey when the president arrives to support him in polls,” Simon Haeder, a political scientist at West Virginia University.
Ryder Cup 2018: United States team shows fighting spirit, maintaining hope of victory in Sunday's singles
After three unilateral sessions, the foursome format taking place on Saturday afternoon could have been what Ryder Cup needed.
The ragging pendulum of momentum is a completely invented sports concept, but one in which the players really believe, and already the most at such competitions as these.
So what's their momentum like at this point?
"They've led by six points, and now they're only leading four, so I think we have a little momentum," Jordan Spieth said.
Europe, of course, leads by four points, while there are still twelve to be scored.
The Americans, Spieth says, however, feel the wind in their sails and have worthy role models, especially in the form of Spieth and Justin Thomas, who played together throughout the day and each of them could boast getting three points out of four.
Spieth is dangerous in all areas and sets an example.
Screams at the entire throat, which are a sign of excitement, became louder and louder during the duration of his round, sinking a key putt and winning the match when he and Thomas scored two holes in two strokes.
His rebounce on the ground, which gave them victory with a score of 15, was met with a similar cry – one that makes it clear that the American team is still in the game.
"It's enough to focus and worry about your own match," Spieth said.
That's all that's left for each of these players.
18 holes to victory.
The only players who have scored more points in the last two days than Spieth and Thomas were Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood, an undeniable Ryder Cup story.
A strange but charming pair of the European team won four out of four and were not mistaken.
“Moliwood” is the only couple who didn’t score bogey on Saturday afternoon, but also escaped the bogeys on a Saturday morning, Friday afternoon, and back nine on Friday morning.
The way of playing and how their energy seems to flow both to and from the noisy crowd proves that these players should be defeated on Sunday, and that there is no better player than Fleetwood or Molinari, who could seal the potential victory of the European team at Le Golf National.
It would be best if they played at the same time on different pits.
However, it is too early to talk about the victory of the European team.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson quickly played Sergio Garcia, the fourth-ball hero when he was paired with Alex Noren.
Bogey and the two-double-double-double on the front nine dug the Spaniard and Swede into a hole they would never get out of.
There's no one on Sunday who can help you get out of the hole.
Fourball and foursome formats are as fascinating due to interactions between couples, the advice they give, the advice they don’t give, and how in an instant there may be a change of strategy.
Europe has so far played better as a team and has taken a significant advantage on the last day, but the foursome session has also shown that the U.S
Europe with a score of 10-6 takes the lead in the Ryder Cup's final day.
Europe with a significant advantage on the last day of Ryder Cup after the win with the result of 10-6 Saturday's fourball and foursom matches with the United States.
Inspired duo Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari launched a charge with two victories over the struggling Tiger Woods, boosting their previous score at Le Golf National to four points.
Thomas Bjorn's European team trying to preserve the trophy he lost at Hazeltine two years agore dominated the unaimed American team in the morning fourball format, winning the series with a 3-1 result.
The U.S. put up more resistance in foursome format, winning two matches but couldn't make up for losses.
To preserve the trophy, Jim Furyk's team must score eight points from 12 singles, which will take place on Sunday.
Fleetwoode is Europe's first runner-up to score four points in a row, while along with Molinari (this team was named "Molliwood" after a sensational weekend) are just the second pair in Ryder Cup's history, having scored four points in their four opening games.
After crushing Woods and Patrick Reed in fourball format, they beat the pumped-out Woods and American debutant Bryson Dechambeau with an even more empathetic 5&4 score.
Woods, who struggled for two Saturday games, occasionally showed glimpses of genius, but so far has lost 19 of his 29 games in fourball and foursome formats, seven in a row.
Justin Rose, who did not take part in the morning fourball format, returned to Henrik Stenson in foursome format to defeat 2&1 Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, who ranked 1 and 3.
The European team, however, did not all go their way on this pleasant, cold day in a field southwest of Paris.
Three-time lead winner Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas set a benchmark for the Americans, scoring two points on Saturday.
They won a decisive victory with a 2&1 score over Spaniard Jon Rah and Ian Poulter in fourball format and came back later to beat Poulter and Rory McIlroy with 4&3 a foursome format, losing two opening holes.
Only twice in Ryder Cup's history has it happened for the team to make up for a four-point loss by starting the singles, although it would only be enough for Furyk's team to keep the trophy.
Taking, however, before two days second place, Sunday's counter-attack seems rather unlikely.
Northern Korea says "state-of-the-line no" unilateral disarmament without trust.
North Korea's foreign minister said Saturday during a speech at the U.N. that the failure to lift sanctions only exacerbates North Korea's lack of confidence in the United States and there is no way that the country has unilaterally given up its nuclear weapons under such circumstances.
Ri Yong Ho said at the Annual General Assembly that North Korea took "significant measures of goodwill" last year, such as halting nuclear and missile tests, dismantling nuclear test sites and a commitment to non-proliferation of weapons and nuclear technology.
“But we don’t see any specific response from the U.S.,” he said.
“Without confidence in the United States, there will be no guarantee of our national security, and under such circumstances there is no unilateral disarmament of North Korea.”
While Ri reiterated his well-known North Korean complaints about Washington’s dislike of “gradual” approach to denuclearization, under which North Korea would be rewarded for gradually taking action, its statement appeared to be significant because it did not reject unilateral denuclearization, as happened in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first-ever summit between the incumbent president of the United States and North Korea's leader on June 12, when Kim Jong Un pledged to work to "denuclearize the Korean peninsula," and Trump pledged North Korea's security assurances.
North Korea expected a formal end to the Korean War (1950-1953), but the U.S. delegation announced that Pyongyang must first give up nuclear weapons.
Washington has also resisted calls to ease harsh international sanctions against North Korea.
"The United States insists on "denuclearization in the first place" and increases the pressure through sanctions to achieve its goal in a forced manner, opposing even the signing of a "declaration to end the war," Ri said.
“The belief that sanctions can bring us to our knees are the glooms of people who act towards us in an ignorant way.
However, the problem is that the failure to abolish sanctions exacerbates our distrust.”
Ri did not mention plans for a second summit between Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump, which the leader of the United States spoke about at the beginning of the week at the U.N. forum.
Instead, the minister highlighted three meetings between Kim Jong Un and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the last five months, after which he added: "If South Korea, rather than the United States, party to denuclearization did not occur at the time."
Mim this, the tone of Ri's speech was completely different compared to last year, when he said during the U.N. General Assembly that targeting North Korea's missiles on U.S. territory is inevitable after "Mr Evil President" Trump (Mr. Bad President Trump) called Kim Jong Un a "rocket man" on a suicidal mission.
This year at the U.N. forum Trump, which last year threatened the "total destruction" of North Korea, praised Kim Jong Un for his courage in taking steps for disarmament, but he also said he still needed to put a lot of work into, and he also stated that until North Korea was deprived of nuclear weapons, sanctions would not be lifted.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn't set a timeframe for it, saying, "Whether it takes two years, three years or five months, it doesn't matter."
China and Russia say the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the steps taken.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday that “Enforcing security council sanctions must continue and continue until we determine full, final and verified denuclearization.”
Since 2006, the Security Council has unanimously tightened sanctions against North Korea in an attempt to quell Pyongyangu's nuclear and ballistic missile program.
Pompeo met Ri outside the U.N. General Assembly forum and later said he would revisit Pyongyang next month in preparation for his second summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but his last visit cannot be called a success.
He left Pyongyang in July, saying progress had been made, after which within a few hours North Korea condemned him for "making gangster demands."
North Korea pledged this month at a meeting with Moon Jae to dismantle a missile testing site and a nuclear complex if the U.S. takes "appropriate measures."
He said Kim Jong Un told him that the "appropriate measures he cares about are security guarantees that Trump has pledged to provide in Singapore, and take steps to normalize relations with Washington."
Harvard students attend a course on adequately lengthy rest
A new course at Harvard University held this year has provided all students with more sleep, which was aimed at tackling a growing macho culture involving learning by "all-night caffeine-powered students."
Academic students from the best university in the world often have no idention of the basics of taking care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, has developed a course that he believes is the first of its kind in the United States.
To create the course, he was inspired by giving a speech about the impact of sleep deprivation on science.
“At the end of the speech, one girl approached me and said, ‘what’s only being said to me now that I’m in my senior year?”
She stated that no one had ever told her about the importance of sleep, which surprised me," The Telegraph said.
During the course, which took place for the first time this year, it is explained to students how good sleep habits help in achieving good academic and sports performance, as well as improving the overall well-being of students.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and chief executive of the university's health service, said the course was decided to introduce after it was found that students had suffered from a severe lack of sleep within a week.
An hour-long course includes a number of interactive tasks.
One section includes an image of a dorm room where students click coffee cups, curtains, exercises and books for information about the effects of caffeine and light and how sleep deprivation affects athletic performance, as well as how important it is to maintain sleep regularity.
In another section, participants learn how long-term lack of sleep can increase the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancers.
A campus map with interactive icons encourages participants to reflect on their daily routine.
“We know this won’t result in an immediate change in student behavior.
But we believe they have a right to know — just as everyone has the right to know the health effects of cigarette smoking,” Professor Czeisler added
A culture of pride from "all-night science" still exists, he said, adding that modern technology and ever-growing pressure on students means an ever-increasing problem of lack of sleep.
Ensuring enough good-quality sleep should be a “secret weapon” of students that combats stress, exhaustion and anxiety, and even avoids gaining weight, because lack of sleep causes the brain to go into starvation, making the student constantly hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old Californian studying chemical and physical biology, assisted Professor Czeisler in developing the course after taking one of his classes last year during his freshman year at Harvard.
He said the course opened his eyes and inspired him to push to introduce a course for the entire campus.
The next step according to him is to ask all postgraduate students to complete a similar degree program before moving on to a competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler recommended that students set an alarm reminding them when you need to go to sleep and when you need to get up, as well as to be aware of the detrimental impact of “blue light” emitted by electronic device screens and LED lighting, which can upset the circadian rhythm and lead to problems with falling asleep.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Menga gets Gerrard's team
Rangers once again suffered during a away fight in Livingston when Dolly Menga with his shot led to the defeat of the dismantled Steven Gerrard's dismantled team, yielding a 1-0 score.
The Ibrox Stadium team wanted to record its first victory since the February 4-1 triumph over St Johnstone, but Gary Holt's team beat Gerrard for the second time in 18 games, causing his team a difference of eight points against Ladbrokes Premiership - Hearts.
Menga scored a goal seven minutes before the end of the first half, and the Rangers line-up showing a lack of commitment didn't look like he was going to improve.
While Rangers recorded a drop in sixth place, Livingston climbed third — just behind Hibernian at the loss of one goal.
The Rangers team may be in more trouble due to having to bear the wound of Liner referee Calum Spence after he was hit with an object thrown from the guest stands.
Gerrard made eight changes to the team, allowing him to overtake Ayr and move on to the Betfred Cup semi-finals.
Holt, in turn, went through with the same eleven Livingston, who took a point to the Hearts team last week, being delighted with the way his well-trained team tired at every step of his opponents.
Rangers may dominate possession of the ball, but it was the Livingston team that did more with the ball once they got it.
They should have scored in just two minutes, when Menga's first line-up of the ball allowed Scott Pittman to face Allan McGregor's goal, but the midfielder did not take his chance.
Keaghan Jacobs then addressed a deep free kick to Captain Craig Halkett, but his team-mate Alan Lithgow playing in the defending position could only stay in the back position.
The Rangers regained control in the third quarter, but it looked as if they had more hope than believing in their game.
Alfredo Morelos certainly acknowledged that he deserved a penalty kick fifteen minutes before the end of the game when he and Steven Lawless bumped into each other, but referee Steven Thomson dispelled the Colombian's claim.
Rangers managed to fire only two goals in the first half, but former Ibrox goalkeeper Liam Kelly was barely concerned about Lassana Coulibala's head and Ovie Ejaria's faint shot.
While Livingston's 34-minute opening goal may have been opposed to the course of the game, no one can deny that they deserved him for the very effort put into getting it.
Again, Rangers couldn't handle the deep fixed fragment of the game made by Jacobs.
Scott Arfield didn't react when Declan Gallagher threw the ball into Scott Robinson, who retained calm, giving to Menga for a simple finish of the action.
Gerrard was not idle at half-time and replaced Coulibaly with Ryan Kent—which almost immediately made an impact on the winger who threw the ball into Morelos, but the impressive Kelly managed to block it, coming from his position.
Livingston continued to drag guests into the game exactly as they like when Lithgow and Halkett made long passes one after the other.
Holt's team may have increased the lead in the final minutes, but McGregor pawned well in front of Jacobs, after which Lithgow dismissed the game from the corner.
Rangers' reserve, Glenn Middleton, once again hoped for a penalty kick as he tangled with Jacobs, but Thomson turned his gaze again.
Almanac: Geiger's meter inventor
And now the page from our “Sunday Morning” Almanac: September 30, 1882 — 136 years ago... the day the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method to detect and measure radioactivity that eventually led to the formation of a device known as a Geiger counter.
Since then, Geiger’s counter has become both an element of science and pop culture, such as the 1950 film “Bells of Coronado,” starring these seemingly unlikely cowboy-scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man: “What the hell is it?”
Rogers: “It’s a Geiger counter that is used to locate radioactive minerals such as uranium.
When you put on these headphones, you’ll hear the effects of atoms emitted by radioactive minerals.”
Evans: “You can hear something right now!”
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name has survived to this day.
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to "see" hostile cells.
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” hostile cells and destroy them
The vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize enemy cells as part of treatment.
This method consists in separating immune cells from the patient and changing them in the laboratory.
Then they can “see” a protein common to many cancers. Then they are re-injected into the body.
The trial vaccine shows promising results in patients with different types of cancers.
In one woman treated with an immune-treating vaccine to recognize enemy cells, ovarian cancer atrophy was observed for more than 18 months.
The method consists in separating immune cells from the patient, changing them in the laboratory, so that they can “see” a protein common to many cancers called HER2, and then re-injecting these cells to the patient.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda in Maryland said, "Our results indicate that this vaccine is promising very well."
HER2 “drives the growth of several types of cancers”, including breast, ovarian, lung and colorectal cancer, Professor Berzofsky explained.
A similar approach of taking immune cells from patients and “teaching” their location of cancer cells worked in the treatment of some type of leukemia.
Kanye West embarks on a pro-Trump diatribe on Saturday Night Live, with a MAGA cap on its head.
It didn't go very well.
Kanye West was "hugged" at the studio over the course of Saturday's Saturday Night Live show after praising U.S. U.S. President Donald Trump and said he would run for office in 2020.
After performing his third song titled Ghost Town that night, during which he wore a hat with the inscription "Make America Great," he conducted an argument against the Democrats and re-expressed his support for Trump.
“Whenever I talk to white people, I hear, ‘How can you like Trump? He’s a racist!”
“If I was concerned about racism, I would have moved out of America a long time ago,” he said.
The SNL's program began with a sketch with Matt Damon, in which the Hollywood star mocked Brett Kavanaugh's testimony filed before a Senate judiciary on allegations of sexual assault brought by Christine Blasey Ford.
Even though it wasn't streamed, footage of West was uploaded to social media by comedian Chris Rock.
It's not known if Rock wanted to mock West by posting the post.
In addition, West complained to the audience that he had problems with his hat behind the scenes.
“They looked at me behind the scenes.
They told me not to come out with that hat on my head.
They've caught up with me!
And then they say I have no choice," he said, "The Washington Examiner.
West continued, “Are you saying I don’t have a choice?” adding, “I’m going to put on a supermena cape, so you won’t be able to tell me what to do. Do you want the world to move forward?
Let’s accept ourselves.”
His words twice provoked “booming” to the audience, and members of the SNL team looked embarrassed – reports Variety. A person in the studio during these events: “In the studio there was grave silence.”
West was invited to the show in place of singer Ariana Grande, whose ex-boy, rapper Mac Miller, had died a few days earlier.
West intrigued many with the performance of the track I Love it in the disguise of a Bottle of Perrier water.
West got support from Conservative TPUS group boss Candace Turner posted the following tweet: "To one of the bravest: THANK YOU FOR PUTTING YOURSELF TO THE MAFIA."
TV host Karen Hunter on her Twitter account said West simply "was himself and it's absolutely wonderful."
“However, I will NOT reward a person (by purchasing her music or clothes or supporting their ‘arts’), who, I think, accepts and spreads an ideology harmful to my community.
He's free.
We are also free," she added.
In front of the show, the rapper announced on Twitter that he had changed his name and that he was now "formally called Kanye West."
However, he's not the first artist to change his name — he follows in the footsteps of Diddy, also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
A friendly rapper, Snoop Dogg, adopted the name Snoop Lion, and the music legend, Prince, changed his name into a symbol, and then an artist formerly known as Prince.
Accusation of attempted manslaughter in Belfast restaurant stabbing
A 45-year-old man has been charged with attempted murder after he stabbed a man at a restaurant in eastern Belfast on Friday.
The incident happened in Ballyhackamore, police reported.
The defendant is due to appease on Monday before a settlement court in Belfast.
The charges will be investigated by the prosecutor's office.
Game of Thrones star Kit Harington raises the issue of toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is known for his role as Jon Snow in a medieval HBO television series "Game of Thrones."
The 31-year-old actor struck the stereotype of a macho hero, saying that showing such roles on the screen causes young boys to often think they had to play tough guys to be respected.
Giving an interview to Sunday Times Culture, Kit stated that "something went wrong" and wondered how to deal with the problem of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo era.
Kit, who recently married his partner from the Game of Thrones set, 31-year-old actress Rose Leslie, acknowledged that he felt "quite confident," raising the issue.
“Personally, I’m feeling pretty confident at this point — where we made a mistake about masculinity?” he said.
“What did we teach men during their adolescence, from the perspective of the problem we’re dealing with right now?”
Kit believes that the development of toxic masculinity may be partly responsible television due to the depiction of very masculine characters.
He continued: "What attitudes are innate, and how learned?
What he learns on TV and on the streets, what makes young boys begin to think that they have to stand on a certain side to be a man?
I think this is one of the most important issues of our time – what can be done to change this?
It’s clear that things aren’t okay with young men.”
In the interview, he also confessed that he would no longer star in any prequel or sequel Game of Thrones after completing the series the following year, saying he had "ended up with battlefields and horses."
From November, Kit will star in Sam Shepard's "True West," which is a story about a cinematographer and his brother, a thief.
The actor recently revealed that he considers getting to know his wife Rose as the best thing that happened to him in Game of Thrones.
“On set, I met my wife, which provided me with my future family and my new life,” he said.
In this award-winning Emmy, fantasy series Rose played Ygritte — an object of love interests of a character played by Kit, Jon Snow.
The couple married in June 2018 on the grounds of Leslie's family estate in Scotland.
HIV/Aids: China reports 14 percent increase in new cases
China has announced a 14 percent increase in the number of citizens living with HIV and suffering from AIDS.
More than 820 000 people are affected, according to the country's health workers.
In the second quarter of 2018 alone, about 40 000 new cases were reported.
The vast majority of new cases were sexually transmitted, a change from the past.
Traditionally, HIV spreads rapidly in some parts of China as a result of a transfusion of infected blood.
However, the number of people infected in this way has been almost completely reduced, Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, year-over-year the number of people living with HIV and suffering from AIDS in China has increased by 100,000 people.
Sexually transmitted HIV is a serious problem in China's LGBT community.
Homosexualism was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is widespread.
Taking into account the conservative values of the country, the study estimates that 70–90% of men having sex with men will eventually marry a woman.
Many cases of transmission of the disease are due to insufficient protection when having sex in these relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has ensured universal access to HIV drugs as part of efforts to combat this problem.
Maxine Waters denies that one of her team members has leaked data from Republican senators — “dangerous lies” and “conspiracy theory.”
U.S. Home Member Maxine Waters on Saturday dismissed allegations that a member of her team posted the personal details of three Republican senators on Wikipedia pages of the aforementioned lawmakers.
A member of the Los Angeles-based Democrat Party said the accusations were put forward by experts and websites linked to "ultra-Right wings."
"Lies, lies and even more nefarious lies," Waters said in a Twitter statement.
The public information reportedly included U.S. U.S. Senators Lindsey Graham from South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch from Utah.
It was posted by an unknown person on Capitol Hill during a Senate panel hearing on allegations of sex offenses against Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
The spill occurred some time after Kavanaugh's candidacy was challenged by three senators.
Conservative sites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that the IP address identifying the source of the posts was linked to the Waters cabinet and had published information about the Waters team member, Hill reported.
"This unfounded allegation is completely false and it's a one hundred percent lie," Waters continued.
“A member of my team – whose identity, personal information and security was compromised as a result of these fraudulent and false allegations — was in no way responsible for the leakage of this information.
This unfounded allegation is completely false, and it is a one hundred-percent lie.”
Waters's statement quickly drew criticism online, including from Ari Fleischer — a former White House spokesperson.
"Such a way of denial is bad," Fleischer wrote.
“This suggests that she doesn’t have the temperament to be a Member of Congress.
When someone is accused of something they didn't do, they can't be angry.
He can't provoke.
It cannot question the motives of the person making the allegations.
He has to stay calm for that.”
Fleischer seemed to compare Waters' reaction to criticism of the Democrats against Judge Kavanaugh, which was accused of displaying excessive anger during Thursday's interrogation.
Omar Navarro, the Republican candidate running in the mid-term election to replace Waters, also expressed his thoughts on Twitter.
"That's a big deal, if that's true," he tweeted.
In her statement, Waters said her cabinet had notified "the competent authorities and law enforcement agencies of these fraudulent accusations."
“The perpetrators will be unmasked and held accountable for all of their actions that pose a threat to any of my team members,” she said.
Johnny English: Knockout is a spy parody with the weakened Rowan Atkinson
Looking for references to Brexit in every new film with a British thread has already become the norm. This seems to apply to a new part of the action and comedy film series “Johnny English”, which began in 2003 with the film “Johnny English” and has returned to cinema screens in 2011 with the film “Johnny English: Reactivation”.
Will a self-jimmy satire on how hopeless we are become the country's new export commodity?
In any case, eternally astonished, characterized by a rubber face, incompetent Johnny English was again licensed to screw up — his name more than anything else signals that it was an elaborate, comic-oriented product intended for non-English-speaking viewers.
Of course, he's a silly secret agent who, despite his bizarre tastes for smoothie glamour, has a bit from Inspector Clouseau, a pinch of Jasio Fasola and a cape from this guy adding one note to the melody of the Chariots of Fire theme at the opening ceremony of the 2012 London Olympics.
In the past, he was also a traveler and a world man-secret — Atkinson once starred in Barclaycard’s forgotten TV commercials, in which he left chaos.
In the latest Johnny English movie, there are one or two moments worthy of attention.
I really liked the scene in which Johnny English approached the helicopter, being dressed in medieval armor, while his helmet was rhythmically struck by rotor blades.
On the screen you can clearly see Atkinson’s gift for physically playing comedy, but the sense of humor seems to be quite low-fly and unnecessary, especially since nowadays also “serious” film brands, such as 007 and Mission Impossible, offer comedy elements as part of the film.
Humorous elements seem to be aimed at children rather than adults – in my other, the crazy adventures of Johnny English are not as ingenious and coherent as the gags in Atkinson’s mute films about Jasie Beans.
It's always been a time-to-date assumption that Britain is in serious trouble.
A cyberhacker penetrates the British super-secret network of spies, revealing the identity of all British agents operating in the field — much terrified of an agent in the duty, Kevin Eldon, which unfortunately played a small role.
This is the last drop in the spell of the prime minister’s bitterness, which is a pompous, wasted figure already suffering from a nervous breakdown caused by a lack of political popularity: Emma Thompson is doing everything she can, taking on the role of Theresa May, but the scenario doesn’t give her the opportunity to develop her wings.
Her advisers from the intelligence agency inform her that due to the disclosure of the identity of all active spies, she will have to return one of the retired agents to service.
And that means an inept Johnny English himself, currently employed as a teacher at some elegant school, giving unofficial lessons on how to become an undercover agent — a couple of nice gags while giving espionage lessons through English.
English is summoned to Whitehall for the briefing, reconnecting with his former midfielder Bough, played again by Ben Miller.
Bough is now a married man associated with the commander of a submarine — a cheerful but somewhat tired character played by Vicki Pepperdine.
So Batman and Robin's part-timber in Her Majesty's secret service return to action, encountering the beautiful femme fatale Ophelia Bulletova, a character played by Olga Kurylenko.
Meanwhile, the prime minister is dangerously possessed by a charismatic tech billionaire, who says he can solve Britain’s computer problems — it’s the ominous Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough begin their crusade made up of absurd situations: dressed as waiters setting fire to a French restaurant; introducing chaos, sneaking aboard a luxury Volta yacht; and English wreaks havoc when using a virtual reality set in order to get acquainted with the interior of Volta's house.
In the latter sequence, all restrictions disappear, however, despite the nice and loud performance, the whole film is very similar to children's television.
A typical medium.
As with other Johnny English films, I couldn't resist the thought: cann't Britain's film industry give Rowan Atkinson a role that would really suit his talent?
Labour denies that it is developing a plan, which foresees a four-day working week for Britons, for which they will receive remuneration in five days
Jeremy Corbyn's Labour party will consider introducing a radical plan under which Britons will work four days a week, receiving pay in five days.
The party reportedly wants company bosses to pass on to workers the austerities gained through a revolution related to the development of artificial intelligence (AI), giving them an extra day off.
Thanks to this, employees could enjoy a three-day weekend, earning the same amount of salary.
Sources say that this idea will “fit” into the party’s economic agenda and plans to meet the country’s expectations of employees.
Moving into a four-day working week has been endorsed by the Trade Union Congress as a way for workers to take advantage of changing the economy.
A senior Labour member told The Sunday Times: "It is expected that there will be a policy change announcement before the end of this year."
“It won’t happen overnight, but a four-day working week is something that fits the party’s approach to restoring economic balance for the worker’s benefit, as well as the party’s overall industrial strategy.
The Labour Party is not the first party to support such an idea — the Green Party has pledged to introduce a four-day working week during the 2017 election campaign.
At present, however, these plans are not supported by all members of the Labour Party.
A Labour spokesperson said: "A four-day workweek is not party policy and its introduction is not under consideration."
John McDonnell, Chancellor of the shadow cabinet, used last week's Labour Conference to present his vision of a socialist revolution in the economy.
Mr McDonell said he was determined to regain power from "faceless directors" and "speculators" at utility companies.
Chancellor's plans from the shadow cabinet also mean that the current shareholders of water companies may not regain their entire participation, because the Labour government could make "deductions" based on the abuses detected.
He also confirmed plans to place employees on company boards and set up integrative equity funds in order to donate 10 percent of the equity of private sector companies to employees who receive annual dividends of up to 500 pounds.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy respond in the "60 minutes" program to the question of whether the FBI's investigation into Kavanaugh could make them change their mindset
The FBI's investigation into the charges against Judge Brett Kavanaugh delayed the final vote on his Supreme Court nomination by at least a week and prompted a question as to whether the findings of an investigative bureau could prompt Republican senators to withdraw their endorsement.
In an interview conducted Sunday's "60 Minutes," Scott Pelley asked Republican senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham whether the FBI could discover something that might lead to a change in their attitude.
Kennedy was more open-minded than his South Carolina teammate.
“Of course,” Kennedy said.
“Going to the hearing, I spoke to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him after it happened when that allegation came out, and I asked, “Did you do it?”
He was firm, decisive and unequivocal.”
Graham's opinion, however, appears to have been carved in stone.
"I've made a decision about Brett Kavanaugh and a strong allegation is needed to change it," he said.
“Dr. Ford, I don’t know what happened, but I know this: Brett strongly denied it,” Graham added, addressing Christine Blasey Ford.
“None of the people he gives could verify it.
It's been 36 years.
I don’t think anything will change.”
What is the Global Citizen Festival event and has it contributed to reducing poverty in any way?
Next Saturday’s Global Citizen Festival will take place in New York, an annual music event that boasts an extremely impressive line-up of stars and an equally impressive mission – the eradication of poverty in the world.
For the seventh time, the Global Citizen Festival will visit tens of thousands of people coming to Great Lawn in Central Park, not only to enjoy the performances of such stars as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also in order to support the achievement of the most important goal of this event, which is the liquidation of extreme poverty until 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, launched in 2012, is an expansion of the Global Poverty Project, an international group of activists to combat poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting it.
In order to receive a free ticket for this event (unless it was decided to buy a VIP ticket), the participants of the concert had to perform a number of tasks or “actions”, such as volunteering, sending an e-mail to a world leader, making a phone call or other significant actions to help raise awareness about the purpose of eliminating poverty.
But what successes can Global Citizen boast in the 12 years remaining to achieve its goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free concert a good way to convince them to take action, or another case of so-called "clictivism," when people feel like they're doing something important by signing an online petition, or posting a tweet?
Global Citizen says it has recorded more than 19 million “actions” in support of the action since 2011, aiming to achieve many different goals.
He also argues that these actions have helped stimulate world leaders to announce more than US$37 billion worth of commitments and regulations that are expected to impact the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 liabilities and announcements resulting from its activities, under which at least 10 billion dollars have already been paid or financed.
The group estimates that secured funds have so far had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people worldwide.
Some of the key commitments include The Power of Nutrition, a UK partnership of investors and implementers involved in "helping children to reach their full potential," which promised to donate to Rwanda US$35 million to help eliminate malnutrition in the country, after Global Citizen members uploaded more than 4,700 tweets.
“With the support of the British government, donors, national governments and members of Global Citizen, such as you, we can make the social injustice of malnutrition go down in history” — Tracey Ullman, ambassador of Power of Nutrition, during a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that after carrying out more than 5000 actions calling on Britain to improve the quality of nutrition of mothers and children, the government has announced funding for the Power of Nutrition project, which will reach up to 5 million women and children with nutritional problems.
One of the most frequently asked questions on his website, which was “When’s the certainty that extreme poverty can be eradicated?”
“It’s going to be a long and difficult road – sometimes we’re going to stumble and fail.
But just as in the case of the ersty great civil rights movements and the fight against apartheid, we will succeed because together we are stronger.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are among the stars performing at this year's event in New York, hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
The U.S. could use the navy to “lock down” Russian energy exports — Home Secretary
Washington can resort to its navy to prevent an influx of Russian energy into markets, including those in the Middle East
Zinke maintained that Russia’s involvement in Syria, especially where it works at the invitation of a legitimate government, is an excuse to explore new energy markets.
“I believe that the reason they are in the Middle East is to want to mediate energy trading, as is the case in Eastern Europe,” he said.
According to the official, there are ways and means to solve this problem.
"The United States, thanks to its navy, has the ability to make sure that maritime routes remain open and, if necessary, create a blockade in order to ensure that their energy does not hit the market," he said.
Zinke spoke to the participants of an event organized by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-profit group that describes itself as the “voice of the energy consumer” in the United States.
He also compared Washington’s approach to Russia and Iran, noting that they were actually the same.
"Iran's and Russia's economic option is more or less about the use and substitution of fuels," he said, comparing Russia to a "pony who knows one trick" with an economy dependent on fossil fuels.
These statements came at a time when the Trump administration is continuing its mission to increase exports of liquefied natural gas to Europe — which is a much cheaper option for European consumers compared to Russian gas.
To this end, officials of the Trump administration, including U.S. President Donald Trump himself, are trying to convince Germany to withdraw from the implementation of the “inappropriate” Nord Stream 2 gas pipeline project, which according to Trump has made Berlin a “slaver” of Moscow.
Moscow has repeatedly emphasized that the 11-billion-dollar Nord Stream 2 pipeline, which is expected to double the existing capacity of up to 110 billion cubic meters, is purely an economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington’s fervent opposition to the implementation of this project is purely due to economic reasons and is an example of unfair competition.
"We believe that we share the notion that energy cannot be a tool to exert pressure and that consumers should be able to choose suppliers," Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said after a meeting with U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry in Moscow in September.
The position of the United States provoked a violent reaction from Germany, which confirmed its commitment to the implementation of the project.
Germany's leading industrial organisation, the Federation of German Industry (BDI), urged the United States not to interfere in the EU's energy policy and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
“I think it’s a big problem when a third country interferes with our energy supplies,” said Dieter Kempf, head of the Federation of German Industry (BDI), after a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Eligena Warren, a Massachusetts senator, said she would consider running for president in 2020
Elabila Warren, the Massachusetts senator, said Saturday that she would consider running for president after the mid-term election.
During a meeting in Holyoke, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed she was considering her candidacy.
“It’s time for women to go to D.C. and fix a broken government. This also applies to women holding the highest position,” she said.
“After November 6, I will consider my candidacy for the position of president.”
During the meeting, Warren mentioned President Donald Trump, saying she "leads the country in the wrong direction."
"I'm very concerned about what Donald Trump is doing with our democracy," she said.
Warren, without a garden, expressed her criticism of Trump and his Supreme Court candidate, Brett Kavanaugh.
On Twitter Friday, Warren tweeted: "Obviously we need an FBI investigation before voting."
The poll, published on Thursday, however, showed Warren's majority of voters didn't think they should run in 2020.
Fifty-eight percent of Massachusetts voters choosing the "probably" option said the senator shouldn't run, according to a poll conducted by Suffolk University Political Research Center/Boston Globe.
Thirty-two percent supported the candidacy.
The poll showed greater support for the candidacy of former Gov. Deval Patrick — 38 per cent of people supported his potential candidacy, and 48 per cent were opposed to it.
Other well-known names from the democratic camp considered in relation to the potential candidacy in 2020 are former Vice President Joe Biden and Senator Vermont Bernie Sanders.
Biden said he would make an official decision by January, the Associated Press reported.
Sharah Palin talks about Track Palin's post-traumatic stress (PTSD) syndrome during Donald Trump's campaign rally
Track Palin (26 years old) spent a year in Iraq after enlisting the military in September.
On Monday night, he was arrested and charged with domestic violence.
"What my own son is going through, what he has to go through when he returns, I understand other families who are feeling the effects of PTSD and the wounds with which our soldiers are coming back," she tolded an audience at Donald Trump's campaign rally in the city of Tulsa, Oklahoma.
Palin called her son's arrest a "taboo subject," after which she said of her son and other war veterans: "they come back a little changed, they come back hardened, they come back, wondering if they would be shown respect for what their fellow soldiers, airmen and all other members of the military had given from themselves to their country."
He was arrested Monday in Wasilla, Alaska, and was charged with domestic violence against a woman, urging to give false testimony in a report on domestic violence and possession of weapons in an intoxicated state, according to Dan Bennett, a spokesperson for Wasilla Police Department.
18 states and the District of Colombia support the fight against a new asylum policy
Eighteen states and the District of Columbia support a legal challenge to a new U.S. policy that refuses to grant asylum to victims fleeing a gang or domestic violence.
Representatives of 18 states and the district filed an amicus curiæ letter in Washington on Friday to support an asylum seeker struggling with this policy, according to NBC News.
The plaintiff's full name in the lawsuit Grace v. Sessions, which the American Civil Liberties Union filed against federal politics in August, has not been released.
She said her partner "and his violent sons from the gang" had abused her, but U.S. officials rejected her asylum application on July 20.
She was arrested in Texas.
Prosecutors of the above-mentioned states supporting Grace described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, where there are a large number of asylum seekers in the U.S., as countries facing widespread problems with gangs and domestic violence.
A new U.S. asylum policy overturned the 2014 Board of Immigration Appeals decision that allowed undocumented immigrants fleeing domestic violence to apply for asylum.
Karl Racine, Attorney General of the District of Columbia, said in a Friday statement that the new policy "ignores the state, federal and international law in force for decades."
"Federal law requires that all asylum applications be considered on the basis of specific facts and circumstances, and such a prohibition violates this principle," as written in the amicus curiæ letter.
Prosecutors further argued in a letter that policies denying migrants entry would harm the U.S. economy, arguing that they could become entrepreneurs and "constitute the necessary workforce."
Attorney General Jeff Sessions in June ordered immigration judges to stop granting asylum to victims fleeing domestic violence and those caused by gangs.
"Asylum is available to those who are leaving their country of origin due to persecution or fear racially, religiously, nationally or due to belonging to a particular social group or having other political beliefs," Sessions said in his statement of 11 June on asylum policy.
Asylum was never intended to solve all the problems – even serious ones – that people from all over the world face on a daily basis.
Desperate rescue attempts in Palu in a situation where the death toll has doubled in a race aimed at finding survivors
For the survivors, the situation became more and more tragic.
"It's a very intense feeling," said 35-year-old mother Risa Kusuma, comforting her fevered son at an evacuation center in the devastated city of Palu.
“Every minute an ambulance brings bodies.
There is a lack of clean water.”
Residents were seen returning to destroyed homes, searching water-soaked belongings and trying to save anything they could find.
Hundreds of people were wounded, and hospitals, damaged by a 7.5-degree earthquake, quickly overcrowled.
Some of those injured, including Dwi Haris, who suffered spinal and shoulder fractures, rested outside the military hospital in Palu, where patients were being treated outdoors due to severe aftershocks.
Tears filled his eyes, as he recounted a violent earthquake that shooked the room on the fifth floor of a hotel he shared with his wife and daughter.
“There wasn’t time to save yourself.
I think I've been pressed into the rubble of the wall," Haris told the Associated Press, adding that his family had come to the city for the wedding.
“I heard my wife cry out for help, after which her voice quieted down.
I don’t know what happened to her and my child.
I hope they're safe.'
The U.S. ambassador accuses China of "harassment" with propaganda ads.
A week after an official Chinese newspaper posted a four-page advertisement in a U.S. newspaper praising the mutual benefits of U.S.-Chinese traffiction, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the U.S. press to spread propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump last Wednesday referred to a paid addition to China Daily in the Des Moines Regier, the most popular newspaper in Iowa, accusing China of trying to meddle into the U.S. congressional elections on November 6.
Trump's allegation that Beijing was trying to interfere in the U.S. election confirmed what U.S. officials told Reuters that this was a new stage in escalating Washington's campaign aimed at putting pressure on China.
While it is normal for foreign governments to place advertisements promoting trade with their countries, Beijing and Washington are currently escalating a trade war that has led to rise tariffs on reciprocal imports.
China's retaliatory tariffs at the start of the trade war were expected to affect exporters in states such as Iowa, which supported Trump's Republican Party.
Terry Branstad, the U.S. ambassador to China and former long-time governor of Iowa, the chief exporter of agricultural products to China, said Beijing has caused harm to U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
Branstad stated in an article in Sunday’s edition of the Des Moines Register that China “is now doublingly beginning harassment by placing propaganda ads in our own free press.”
“By spreading its propaganda, the Chinese government is benefiting from a respected American tradition of freedom of speech and a free press, placing paid advertising in the Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
However, there will be no voices of opposition at the street kiosk in Beijing, and there will be no real reflection of the divergent opinions that the Chinese may have about the disturbing economic direction chosen by China, given the fact that the media is under the strong control of the Chinese Communist Party," he wrote.
He added that "one of China's best-known newspapers declined to publish" his article, though he did not say which newspaper.
Republicans are alienating each other before mid-term elections women with voting rights through the Kavanaugh scandal, analysts have warned
While a number of leading Republicans are backing and defending Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh in the face of several allegations of sexual assault, the analyst warns that these members will meet during impending mid-term elections with a violent response, particularly from women.
The emotions accompanying this event are unusually great, and the Republican majority already officially wants to vote.
“This can’t be undone,” Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at Maxwell School at the University of Syracuse, said in an article published on Saturday.
Reeher said he had doubts as to whether Senator Jeff Flake (Arizon)'s efforts to carry out a last-minute investigation by the FBI would be enough to appease rage voters.
"Women won't forget what happened yesterday — they won't forget it tomorrow, and they won't forget it until November," Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior adviser and spokeswoman for the progressive MoveOn group, said in a statement.
On Friday morning, protesters chanted "Novembern is Coming," demonstrating in the hall of the Senate because of the Republicans controlling the Judiciary Committee's failure to unwithdress Kavanaugh's nomination despite testimony from Dr. Christine Blasey Ford.
"The level of democratic enthusiasm and motivation will go beyond the scale," Stu Rothenberg, an unbiased political analyst, told one of the news sites.
“Humans say he’s been tall, and it’s true.
But it may be higher, especially among undecided women from suburbs and younger voters between the ages of 18 and 29 who, while they don't like the president, often don't vote."
Even prior to a public testimony, Ford, in which she detailed her allegations of sexual assault against a candidate to the Supreme Court, analysts suggested that if Republicans had decided to approve their candidacy, it might have come to a violent response.
"The Republican party needs to deal with quite a mess," Michael Steele, former president of the Republican National Committee, told NBC News earlier last week.
“It’s not just about a committee vote or a final vote, or whether Kavanaugh will be elected, it’s also about the way Republicans have dealt with this situation and how they’ve treated Ford,” Guy Cecil, director of U.S. Priorities, a group that helps elect the Democrats, stated on one news channel.
However, the Americans seem to be somewhat divided as to who they believe in the face of Ford and Kavanaugh’s testimony, with the latter having slightly greater support.
A new YouGov poll shows that 41 percent of respondents strongly or rather believed Ford's testimony, while 35 percent said they strongly or rather believed Kavanaugh's testimony.
In addition, 38% of respondents said Kavanaugh probably or certainly lied when testifying, while only 30% found the same about Ford.
After Flake's pressure, the FBI launched an investigation introduced Ford's allegations, as well as at least one other accuser, Deborah Ramirez, as reported by The Guardian.
Ford testified last week under oath before the Senate Judicial Committee that Kavanaugh, under the influence of alcohol, assaulted her at the age of 17.
Ramirez claims the Supreme Court nominee showed her genitals during admission during her studies at Yale University in the 1980s.
The creator of the World Wide Web is planning to launch a new Internet in order to compete with Google and Facebook platforms.
Tim Berners-Lee, creator of the World Wide Web, is launching a startup that is expected to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google platforms.
Inrupt’s latest technology legend project is an open-source platform-based company, Solid, owned by Berners-Lee.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and what people have access to specific information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the purpose of creating Inrupt was "domination of the world."
“We have to do it now,” he said, referring to the startup.
“Historic moment has come.”
The app uses Solid platform technology to allow people to create their own “online store with personal data” or POD.
It may contain contact lists, to-do lists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify available in one browser and at the same time.
A unique feature of an online store with personal data is that it is entirely up to the user who has access to what information.
The company calls it a "personal empowerment through data."
According to the company’s CEO, John Bruce, Inrupt’s idea is to provide resources, processes and the right skills to help make the Solid platform available to all people.
Currently, the company includes Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by ICBM, several developers hired to work on the project, and a volunteer development community.
Beginning this week, technology developers around the world were able to create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on Inrupt's website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team don't talk to "Facebook and Google about whether to make a total change, which day by day will turn all their business models upside down.
“I don’t ask their permission.”
In a post published on the Medium platform on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that "Inrupt's mission is to provide commercial energy and the ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of a new network built based on the Solid platform."
In 1994, Berners-Lee changed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has contributed a lot to the debate about net neutrality.
Including Inrupt Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
“I’m extremely excited to think about the next era in the development of the network,” Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: decoration of the clergyman with the Cross of Victoria during World War I
The memory of England's only clergyman, which during World War I as a combatant was awarded the Victoria Cross, was commemorated 100 years later in his hometown.
Lt. Reverend Bernard Vann was decorated on September 29, 1918 for an assault on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
He was killed by a sniper four days later, having never learned that he had been awarded Britain's highest military order.
On Saturday, his two grandchildren unveiled a commemorative stone during a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Vann, said the unveiling of the stone exactly on the 100th anniversary of his grandfather's decoration had an "extremely symbolic character."
According to the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Colonel Vann led his battalion through the Saint-Quentin Canal "in a very dense fog and under severe shelling from field cannons and machine guns."
He then rushed to the line of fire, and with “greatest courage” led her forward, then single-handedly launched an attack on the position of the field cannons, neutralizing three of them.
Colonel Vann was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just over a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann (72) said his grandfather's actions were "something I would never be able to live up to, but at the same time something humble."
Together with his brother, Dr. James Vann, he laid a wreath after the end of a parade led by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said he felt "very honored to take part in the parade," and added that "the courage of the true hero manifests itself in the support he receives from many."
MMA fans didn't sleep all night to watch Bellator 206, instead they got Peppa's Pig.
Imagine you didn’t sleep all night to watch the Emotious Bellator 206 and ultimately not be able to see the main fight.
Gala's show at San Jose featured 13 fights, including six on the home card, and was shown live overnight in the UK on Channel 5.
At 6 a.m., as Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing to fight, viewers in the UK were shocked by changing the show to Peppa's Pig.
Some were disappointed after they didn't sleep until the early hours of the morning specifically to see the fight.
One fan on Twitter described swapping for a children's cartoon as "something a sick joke."
"Under a government regulation, content of this type is inappropriate so that it can be released at 6 a.m., making it necessary to change to a children's program," said Dave Schwartz, Bellator's senior vice president of marketing and communications, when asked about the broadcast.
“Peppa’s pig, yes.”
Bellator's president Scott Coker has said they will refine the timetable to include UK viewers in the future.
"When I'm thinking about a repeat of the situation, I think we'll be able to fix it," Coker said.
“But it’s Sunday six in the morning, so we won’t be able to deal with it until Sunday according to our time, and Monday according to their time.
But we're working on it.
Believe me, when there was a change of program, we were inundated by a lot of comments, none of which sounded friendly.
We were trying to fix it... we thought it was a technical glitch.
But it wasn't a glitch, it was just a legal issue.
I can promise that such a situation will not happen again.
We will limit the program to five fights instead of six — i.e. to the standard amount. Too much we wanted to please the fans and, unfortunately, we overdo it.
It’s an unfortunate situation.”
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt 'worse' because of sexuality
Tom Daley, an Olympic water jumper, says he grew up feeling inferior to everyone else because of his sexuality, but it gave him the motivation to succeed.
The 24-year-old said he didn't realize that not everyone was like him until he went to high school.
On the Desert Island Disces program of Radio 4 hosted by Lauren Laverne, he said he was talking about gay rights to give others "hope."
He also said he would become a parent made him care less about winning the Olympics.
The usual host of this program, Kirsty Young, took several months off due to the disease.
Appearing in the first-time show led by Laverne, Daley said that growing up, he felt "less valuable" than others because "concomitantly liking boys and girls wasn't socially acceptable."
He said, “To this day, this sense of lesser value and being different is something that gives me the strength to succeed.”
He said he wanted to prove he was “worth something” so as not to disappoint everyone when they finally found out about his sexuality.
The two-time Olympic bronze medalist has become a high-profile activist of the LGBT community and used his performance at this year's Commonwealth Games in Australia to urge more countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said he had decided to express himself because he felt happy to be able to live openly, without any implications, and wanted to “hope” in this way.
The three-time world champion said that falling in love with a man — American filmmaker Dustina Lance Black, whom he met in 2013 — “surprised him.”
Daley married an Oscar winner (which is 20 years older than him) last year, pointing out that such an age difference has never been a problem for him.
“When you go through so much at such a young age,” he meant his first Olympics at the age of 14 and his father’s death from cancer three years later, “he said it was difficult for him to find someone of the same age who had experienced similar ups and downfalls.”
The couple became the parents of a boy named Robert Ray Black-Daley in June. Daley said his perception of certain things had completely changed.
“Last year, the most important thing for me was winning a gold medal,” he said.
“And you know what? There are more important things than Olympic gold medals.
“My Olympic gold medal is Robbie.”
His son bears the same name as his father Robert, who died at the age of 40 in 2011 after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said his father didn't accept that he was dying, and one of the last things he asked about was whether they already had tickets for the 2012 London Olympiad — he wanted to have a front-row seat.
“I couldn’t tell him, ‘You won’t be with us to be in the front row, Dad,” he said.
“I held his hand when he stopped breathing. It wasn’t only when he stopped breathing and died that I realized he wasn’t invincible,” he said.
The following year, Daley took part in the 2012 Olympics and won bronze.
“I knew this is what I’ve been dreaming about all my life — jumping into the water in front of a crowd of people at the Olympics — it’s been the best feeling possible,” he said.
It also inspired him to choose the first song — Proud performed by Heather Small — which accompanied him in preparation for the Olympic Games and which still gives him goosebumps.
The Desert Island Discs programme airs on BBC Radio 4 every Sunday at 11:15 a.m BST.
Mickelson's lack of form on the bench during Saturday's Ryder Cup
U.S. Phil Mickelson will set a record on Sunday when he plays his 47th match in the Ryder Cup, but he'll have to work on his form so that he doesn't leave his mark.
Mickelson, playing for the twelfth time in an event taking place every two years, was sent back to the bench by Captain Jim Furyk for the duration of Saturday's fourball and foursome games.
Instead of being at the center of the action, as was often the case on the United States team, the five-time main winner had to spend the day cheering on the team and working to improve his game in the hope of fixing the reason for his referral to the bench.
The 48-year-old, who has never fired the simplest strokes, even at the height of his career, doesn't work very well on the tight-and-nature Le Golf National, on which erroneous strokes are mercilessly punished by long erouches.
And if the field itself isn't daunting enough for Mickelson, in the ninth game on Sunday, he has to face the precision of the British Open winner - Francesc Molinari, who teamed up with rookie Tommy Fleetwood to win all four games this week.
If the Americans (with a loss of four points at the start of 12 singles) start the game with verve, Mickelson's match could prove extremely crucial.
Furyk expressed confidence in his player – he probably couldn’t do anything else.
“He fully understood his role today, patted me on the back, embraced me with his hand and said that tomorrow he would be ready,” Furyk said.
“He’s very self-confident.
He's in the celebrity gallery and contributed a lot to his bands both in the past and this week.
I didn't think he'd play two games.
I thought there'd be more, but that's how it turned out and that's how we had to do it.
He wants to play all the time, just like everyone else.”
Mickelson will break Nick Faldo's record for Ryder Cup matches played on Sunday.
This could mark the end of his Ryder Cup career, which has never matched his individual achievements.
Mickelson has 18 wins, 20 defeats and seven draws. Furyk said his presence contributed a lot to the team.
"He's funny, sarcastic, witty, mocking and it's good to have him in the team room," he explained.
“I think the younger players had a good time with him this week, which was nice to see.
He contributes so much more than just a game.”
European team captain Thomas Bjorn knows that a big point advantage could soon shrink
Thomas Bjorn, the captain of the European team, knows from experience that a significant point advantage before the start of singles on the last day of Ryder Cup can easily turn into difficult gameplay.
The Dane debuted in 1997 in a match on the Valderrama field, where, together with the accompanying vice-captain Sev Ballesteros, he had a five-pointed advantage over the Americans, but the finish line passed by the shortest possible hair, winning 141⁄2-131⁄2.
“It’s impossible to forget that we had a big advantage on Valderram’s field; we also had a big advantage on the Brookline field, where we lost, and in the case of Valderram we only won by a hair,” Bjorn said, in the picture, after winning the 5-3 Class of 2018 on both Friday and yesterday, which gave the lead 10-6 on the Le Golf National field.
Time's going to tell me and all the team members that it's not over.
You're doing your best tomorrow.
Get out there and do everything right.
The end will be only when points appear on the board.
We have a goal of winning a trophy, and that's what we have to focus on.
“I’ve been focusing on 12 players who are on our side, but we’re also good at people on the opposite side — the best players in the world.”
Bjorn, delighted with how his players have handled themselves on a difficult field, added: "I wouldn't do it any better.
That's different tomorrow, however.
Singles start tomorrow, and that's another issue.
It’s good to play with your partner when everything goes the way, but when you play alone, your golfing skills are tested to the limit.
It's a message you have to convey to the players, it's to give it your all tomorrow.
Your partner also has to give it their all.”
Jim Furyk, unlike Bjorn, will expect their players to perform better alone than they did when playing with their partner, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have scored three points out of four.
Furyk himself experienced the big twists of the last day, being a member of the victorious team on the Brookline field, after which he failed after the European team performed a "miracle on the Medinah field."
"I remember every word," he said in response to the question of how Ben Crenshaw, the captain in 1999, warmed up to the fight of his contestants on his final day.
“Tomorrow we’re playing 12 important games — you’d like to get started at a fast pace, as was the case in Brookline and Medinah.
If that momentum goes one way, it's going to put a lot of pressure on the middle games.
We've set our line-up appropriately and put the guys in a way that would enable us to enchant tomorrow."
Thomas was tasked with counter-attacking, facing Rory McIlroy in the major match, as well as Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter — other players of the European team in the top half of the show.
"I've decided on this group of guys in that order because I think he's going to do it throughout the game," Bjorn said of his singles choices.
A new German warship delayed again
The latest frigate of the German navy should be commissioned in 2014 to replace aging Cold War warships, but this will not happen at least until next year due to faulty deals and rising at an alarming rate, local media reported.
"Rheinland-Pfalz, the main ship of the new Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigate, has been postponed to the first half of 2019, according to Die Zeit, citing the words of a military spokeswoman.
The ship should have been included in the navy in 2014, but as a result of problems that arose after delivery, it was necessary to make changes to this ambitious project.
Four Baden-Wuerttemberg units commissioned by the navy in 2007 will replace aging Bremen-class frigates.
Of course, they will be equipped with a powerful gun, a number of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as technological solutions preventing detection, providing m.in. reduction of radar, infrared and acoustic traces.
Other important features include longer periods between maintenances – the operation of new frigates should be possible for up to two years away from the home ports.
Continuous delays, however, mean that these modern warships, which are intended to enable Germany to respond to situations abroad, will already be obsolete at the start of service.
The wretched frigate F125 made headlines last year when the German navy officially refused to put the ship into operation and returned it to Blohm & Voss yard in Hamburg.
It was the first time the navy had returned the vessel to the shipyard after delivery.
Little was known about the reasons behind the return, but the German media reported on a number of key "flaws in software and hardware" that made the ship useless in the combat mission.
Particularly important were the disadvantages of the software, as Baden-Wuerttemberg ships are to be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors, which is half as much as in the case of older Bremen-class frigates.
It has also emerged that the ship is far too heavy, which reduces its performance and limits the navy's ability to carry out future modernizations.
It is believed that the 7,000-tonne ship "Rheinland-Pfalz" is twice as heavier as ships of similar class used by the Germans during World War II.
In addition to defective equipment, the cost of the entire project, including crew training, also becomes a problem.
It is said to have reached a staggering level of €3.1 billion (US$3.6 billion), while the initial cost amounted to €2.2 billion.
Problems with the commissioning of the new frigates are of particular importance in the light of recent warnings that the strength of the German naval fleet is shrinking.
Earlier this year this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, the head of the German parliament's defence committee, acknowledged that the navy "ends ships capable of use."
He also said that this problem increased over time as a result of the decommission of old units without the provision of replacement vessels.
In addition, he expressed his dissatisfaction with the fact that none of the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates could have been included into the naval fleet.
The National Trust eavesdromes the secret lives of bats
New research carried out on property in the Scottish Highlands area is designed to show how bats use the landscape when acquiring food.
Research results are expected to shed new light on the preservation of these exceptional flying mammals and help target future efforts to protect them.
The research, led by researchers from the National Trust for Scotland, will look at dwarves common and fine, as well as brown bats with long ears and bats from a family of ore nights at Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
In key places around the property, special recorders will be deployed for all-seasonal tracking of bat activity.
NHS staff and volunteers will also carry out field examinations using portable detectors.
The expert audible analysis of all recordings is aimed at determining the frequency of signals sent by bats and how individual species behave.
Next, a habitat map and report will be developed to create a detailed picture of their behaviour on a landscape scale.
Rob Dewar, a nature conservation adviser at NTS, hopes that the results will reveal which habitat areas are most important for bats and how they are used by each of the species.
This information will help to identify the benefits of habitat management work, such as the creation of meadows, as well as the best ways to maintain forest areas for bats and other related species.
Bant populations in Scotland and across the UK have declined significantly over the past century.
They are threatened by construction and development work, which have an impact on perches and habitat loss.
Wind turbines and lighting installations can also pose a threat, as can fly sticks and certain chemicals used in building materials and even domestic cats.
Bats aren’t really blind.
However, due to the nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more helpful than their eyes in catching prey.
They use a complicated echolocation technique to search for worms and obstacles along their way.
The NTS organization, which is responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, takes bats very seriously.
It consists of ten trained experts who regularly carry out examinations, pervert inspections and sometimes even rescue operations.
The organisation has even founded Scotland's first and only dedicated bat reserve at the Threave estate in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight out of ten Scottish bat species.
David Thompson, the estate's manager, says it's the perfect territory for bats.
“Threave has a great area for bats,” he said.
“We have old buildings, lots of old trees and excellent habitats.
However, there are still many unknowns when it comes to bats. The work we’re doing here and in other possessions will help us better understand what they need to grow.”
It emphasizes the importance of inspections in terms of the occurrence of bants before proceeding to carry out maintenance work in estates, because the inadvertent destruction of one peg can kill up to 400 females and youngers, which can lead to the extinction of the entire local population.
Bats are protected — it’s illegal to kill, harass or disturb them, and to destroy their perches.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish Bat Conservation Trust officer, encouraged the public to provide assistance.
She said: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats, and in the case of many species, we don't even know how their populations are doing."
Ronaldo rejects allegations of rape, while his lawyers intend to sue a German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo described the rape allegations against him as "fake news," saying people "want to promote themselves," using his name.
His lawyers intend to sue German news magazine Der Spiegel, which published the allegations.
The Juventus Portuguese striker was accused of raping an American woman, Kathryn Mayorga, in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
According to Friday’s reports, Der Spiegel was supposed to pay a woman 375 000 dollars for silence.
Ronaldo (33 years old), a few hours after announcing the allegations, in an instagram live broadcast aimed at his 142 million followers, he described the reports as "fake news."
“No, no, no, no, no.
“Today’s reports are fake news,” the five-time winner of the gold ball said.
“They want to promote themselves using my name.
Nothing new, sir.
They want to be famous by pronouncing my name, but it's part of my job.
I'm happy and everything's fine," added the footballer with a smile on his lips.
Ronaldo's lawyers are preparing to sue Der Spiegel magazine over the allegations they called "unacceptable reporting of suspicions in the area of privacy protection," Reuters reported.
Lawyer Christian Schertz said the footballer would demand compensation for "moral damage in an amount corresponding to the weight of the breach, which is probably one of the most serious violations of personal rights in recent years."
The alleged incident took place in June 2009 at the Palms Hotel and Casino hotel room in Las Vegas.
After a meeting at Ronaldo's nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga reportedly went to the footballer's room, where he allegedly raped the woman anally, according to documents filed at the Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga claims Ronaldo fell to his knees after the alleged incident and told her that in "99 per cent he's a good guy," which was deceived by that "one percent."
According to documentation, Ronaldo confirmed that the couple had sex, but this was done by mutual consent.
Mayorga also says she went to the police and that photos of her injuries had been taken at the hospital, but she later agreed to an out-of-court settlement because she was "afraid of retaliation" and "public humiliation."
The 34-year-old says she now wants to invalidate the settlement because she still can't forget the alleged incident.
At the time of Ronaldo's alleged assault, he switched from Manchester United to Real Madrid, and moved to Juventus this summer after paying 100 million euros for him.
Brexit: UK 'will forever regret' departure of automakers
Britain "would regret it forever" if, after leaving the European Union, it lost its status as a world leader in car manufacturing, Business Minister Greg Clark said.
He added that he was "compromised" when a Toyota UK official said in an interview with the BBC that if the UK left the EU without a deal, then production at a factory in Burnaston near Derby would be temporarily halted.
“We need an agreement,” Clark said.
A representative of the Japanese car manufacturer said the effects of delays at the border in the event of a no-deal on Brexit could lead to a reduction in jobs.
Burnaston, which produces Auris and Avensis models, produced almost 150 000 cars last year, 90% of which were exported to other European Union countries.
"In my opinion, if the UK leaves the European Union at the end of March without a deal, then there will be a halt to production at our factory," said Marvin Cooke, managing director of Toyota at Burnaston.
Other British automakers are afraid of leaving the EU without agreement on how cross-border trade, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, operates.
For example, BMW intends to close its Mini car factory in Oxford a month after Brexit.
The main concerns are, according to the car manufacturers, about the dangers of the supply chain in the event that the UK leaves the European Union without an agreement.
The Toyota production line operates on a “just-in-time” basis, under which parts reach every 37 minutes from suppliers in both the UK and the EU for custom-made cars.
If the UK leaves the EU without a deal on March 29, there could be obstacles at the border, which industry says could lead to delays and parts shortages.
According to the company’s representative, Toyota wouldn’t be able to withstand more than one day without deliveries to its Derbyshire plant, making production halted.
Clark said Theresa May's Chequers plan for future relations with the EU is "precisely structured in order to avoid control at the border."
"We need an agreement. We want to achieve the best possible agreement that will allow us not only to enjoy the current success, but will also allow us to take proper advantage of this situation," he said on today's BBC Radio 4 programme.
“Not only Toyota’s statements, but also other manufacturers show that we need to be able to continue what made up to a very efficient set of supply chains.”
Toyota's representation has not been able to say how long production will be halted, but warned that in the long term, additional costs will reduce the competitiveness of the plant, which will lead to a reduction in employment.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has been working at Burnaston plant for 24 years and is a member of Unite's trade union board, said the union's members are increasingly concerned: "I know from experience that if there is a decommissioning of jobs, they will never be restored.
A government spokesperson said: "We have put forward a precise and credible plan for our future relationship with the European Union."
Trump's meeting with Rosenstein could be delayed again, according to the White House
Donald Trump's important meeting with Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could be "delayed for another week" because of a relentless fight for supreme court candidate Brett Kavanaugh, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of special accuser Robert Mueller, which explores the possibility of Russian interference in elections, ties between Trump's close associates and Russia, and potential obstruction of justice efforts by the president.
Whether Trump fires deputy attorney-general, thus threatening Mueller's independence, has been a hot topic in Washington for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times newspaper reported that Rosenstein was discussing wearing a wiretap in order to record conversations with Trump, as well as the possibility of removing the president from his post on the basis of the 25th amendment.
Rosenstein denied these reports.
Last Monday, however, he went to the White House in the face of reports that he was about to resign.
The date of the meeting with Trump, which was at the U.N. headquarters in New York at the time, has been set for Thursday.
Trump said he'd rather not fire Rosenstein. There had been a postponement of the meeting to avoid colliding with a hearing by a Senate judiciary committee, during which testimony was given by Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him of sexual abuse, Dr. Christine Blasey Ford.
On Friday, Trump ordered a week-long FBI investigation into the allegations against Kavanaugh, which further delayed the Senate vote.
Trump spokeswoman Sarah Sanders appeared on Fox News on Sunday.
When she was asked about a meeting with Rosenstein, she replied: "A deadline has not yet been set for this meeting, it could take place this week, and it could be postponed by another week, taking into account all events related to the Supreme Court.
We’ll see, I’ll inform the press.”
Few reporters questioned the statement: Sanders hasn't held a White House news conference since September 10.
The show's host, Chris Wallace, asked about the reason for this.
Sanders said that a small number of press conferences were not due to a reluctance to "ostentatious" television journalists, after which she added: "I won't hide that they are "ostentatious."
She then suggested that direct contact between Trump and the press would increase.
"The current President conducts more 'questions and answers' sessions than any other president before him," she said, adding without giving evidence: "We've read the numbers."
Press conferences will continue to take place, Sanders said, but “if journalists have the opportunity to directly ask the president of the United States, this is a much better solution than talking to me.
We try to do this regularly, and in the last few weeks we have done this very often. Press conferences will give way to such sessions, during which it will be possible to talk directly with the president of the United States.”
Trump regularly answers questions when leaving the White House or attending open sessions or press conferences with host dignitaries.
Solo press conferences are rare.
This week in New York, the president may have demonstrated what this is the case, behaving too freely and sometimes bizarrely in front of assembled journalists.
Health minister writes to workers from European Union countries employed at NHS Scotland facing Brexit concerns
The health minister sent a letter to workers from European Union countries employed in the Scottish health service to express their gratitude from the country and ask them not to leave after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, a member of the Scottish parliament, sent the letter for less than six months until Britain left the European Union.
The Scottish Government has already pledged to cover the costs of examining "settled status" applications for EU citizens working in decentralised public services.
Freeman wrote in her letter: "Negotiations on Britain's exit from the European Union have continued in the summer of the summer. The relevant decisions will be taken in the autumn of this year.
The British government has also stepped up preparations for a possible no-deal exit scenario.
I know it's a very disturbing period for all of you.
That is why I would like to reiterate once again how much I value the contribution of every member of staff, regardless of their nationality.
Colleagues from all over the EU and beyond bring valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of the health service and bring benefits to the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is your home and we really want you to stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie undergoes surgery due to head injury
Tennessee State Tigers charger Christion Abercrombie underwent surgery as a head injury in Saturday's match against Vanderbilt Commodores, which ended in defeat for the Tennessee team with a score of 31-27, Mike Organ reported.
Rod Reed, the Tennessee State team's chief coach, told reporters that the player had sustained the injury shortly before the end of the first half.
“He approached the sideline, then fell to the ground,” Reed said.
Coaches and medical staff gave Abercrombie oxygen on the sideline, then placed it on a stretcher and carried it out for further testing.
A Tennessee State representative told Chris Harris of WSMV in Nashville (Tennessee) that Abercrombie was operated at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that "there are no details yet regarding the type/size of the injury," and Tennessee State is trying to determine where the injury occurred.
Abercrombie (a second-year student exempted from representing a college for a year to improve their skills) is playing his first season in the Tennessee State team after being transferred from Illinois.
On Saturday before the end of the match, he had five charges, giving him 18 charges throughout the season.
Foreign buyers will be charged a higher stamp duty when buying property in the UK
Under new Tory plans, buyers from abroad will be charged higher stamp duties for the purchase of real estate in the UK. The funds obtained in this way will be used to help the homeless.
This move is intended to unleash the success of Jeremy Corbyn’s actions aimed at attracting the young electorate.
The increase in stamp duty will cover people who do not pay taxes in the UK.
In this way, the Treasury predicts that it will gain up to 120 million pounds a year to help the homeless.
Theresa May will report today that foreigners buying properties in the UK will be charged a higher stamp duty, and the additional funds obtained in this way will be earmarked to help the homeless.
This move is perceived as an attempt to offset Jeremy Corbyn’s efforts to attract the young electorate with the promises of cheaper housing and to increase the burden on more earners.
Higher stamp duties will apply to individuals and non-tax companies in the UK. Additional measures are intended to assist the government in the fight against homelessness.
In addition to the current stamp duty, including two years ago higher rates for second homes and flats bought for rent, is up to three percent.
The Treasury expects this move to yield as much as 120 million pounds a year.
It is estimated that 13 per cent of newly built properties in London are bought by residents of other countries, leading to rising prices and making it difficult to buy their first home or apartment.
Because a lot of foreigners buy real estate, and spend most of their time outside the country, many wealthy regions—especially the capital—have turned into “guid cities.”
The new rules come in just a few weeks after Boris Johnson's call for a reduction in stamp duties that would help young people buy their first home.
He accused big developers of maintaining high property prices by occupying the property, but its non-utilisation. He also urged Prime Minister May to lift limits on cheap housing in order to eradicate Britain's "housing scandal."
Jeremy Corbyn announced a number of attractive housing reforms, including rental regulation and the end of eviction "without a reason."
He also wants to give local governments greater powers to build new homes.
Theresa May said: "Last year I said that as prime minister I would be committed to rebuilding the British dream of making the life of each successive generation better than the previous one.
It also means repairing our shaky housing market.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and build their lives.
However, it is not right that people who do not live here, as well as foreign companies, can buy real estate here as hard-working British residents.
For too many people, the dream of their own home has become very distant, but the humiliation associated with the need to stay on the street remains too real.”
Jack Ross: 'My biggest ambition is to become a Scotland selector'
Sunderland coach Jack Ross says his "greatest ambition" is to become Scotland's selector at some point.
This forty-two-year-old Scot is committed to working to heal the North-East club, which is currently ranked third in the League One with a three-point loss relative to the best team.
He moved to the Stadium of Light this summer after last season introducing St Mirren back to the Scottish Premiership.
“I wanted to represent my country as a player.
But I only managed to get into the B team," Ross said in an interview with BBC Scotland's Sportsundound.
“As a child, I’ve often watched Scotland’s matches in Hampden with my father, and I’ve always been attracted to it.
However, such a possibility will come only on the condition that I succeed as the coach of the club.”
Among Ross' predecessors as Sunderland coach included Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former coach Alloa Athletic says he doesn't feel intimidated, taking on the role after such illustrious personalities at such a big club, having previously rejected proposals from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
“Currently, success will be measured for me by whether I will be able to bring this club back back to the Premier League.
Taking into account its structure and the facilities at its disposal, there is undoubtedly its place,” he adds.
“It won’t be easy to do that, but I’ll probably feel that I’ve been successful here, only if I succeed.”
Rossa’s coaching career lasted only three years. Before that, he was an assistant coach at Dumbarton and belonged to the Hearts coaching team for 15 months.
He then helped Alloa advance again after relegating into the third division, and in the following season he led St Mirren's team from the bringe of dropping to the championship title.
Jack Ross says he's much more comfortable right now than he did when he played for Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
“That was the real turning point,” he recalls taking the position of Coach Alloa.
“I really believed that leading a team suited me a lot better than just playing.
It's going to sound odd, because as a player I've been doing well, I've made good money and I've achieved some measurable success.
However, playing is a heavy piece of bread.
Every week you have to get through different situations.
They still meet me when it comes to stress and the pressure of the coach's job, but I feel good in this role.
“I’ve always wanted to train others and I’m doing it now. I can say that at the moment I feel the most in place throughout my adult life.”
You can listen to the entire interview at Sportsound on Sunday, September 30 on Scotland radio between 12:00 and 13:00 BST
Research shows that the best time for a beer is 17.30 a.m
The summer heatwave has caused a violent influx of customers into British pubs, but has bounced hiccups at restaurant chains.
Studies have shown that sales in pubs and bars increased by 2.7 percent in July, but fell 4.8 percent in restaurants.
Peter Martin of the consulting firm CGA, which developed the data, says: "Continuous heat and longer-than-expected participation of England in the World Championship caused the results of July to develop similarly to June, when turnover in pubs increased by 2.8 percent, with the difference that restaurant salaries fell even further.
Recorded in June's 1.8-percent decline in sales at restaurants in July grew even further.
Drinking pubs and bars fared by far the best – their increase in sales exceeded the decline recorded in restaurants.
Pubs serving food also suffered in the heat, although not as much as restaurant owners.
It looks like people were just thirsty.
Beverage sales in managed pubs and bars rose 6.6 percent this month, and food fell by three per cent.”
Paul Newman, of RSM's entertainment and tourism analysis company, says: "These results fit into the trend we've seen since the end of April.
The most important factors influencing the volume of consumption “outside the house” remain the weather and large sports or social events.
The fact that restaurant chains are going through difficult times is not surprising, although a decrease in sales of 4.8 percent compared to the previous year will have particularly severe consequences, taking into account the constant pressure to reduce costs.
The long and hot summer came at the least favorable moment for the owners of premises serving food and time will tell whether the slightly more moderate temperatures we had in August will bring so much necessary respite.”
Total sales growth in pubs and restaurants, taking into account those newly opened, amounted to 2.7 percent in July, reflecting a slowdown in brand development.
Coffer Peach Tracker, which monitors sales in the UK’s pubs, bars and restaurants sectors, collects and analyzes performance data from 47 operational groups with a total turnover of more than 9 billion pounds. It sets recognized industry standards.
One in five children has secret social media accounts he hides from his parents
Studies have shown that one in five children — some as young as 11 years old — have secret accounts on social networks that they hide from their parents and teachers
The survey conducted among 20 thousand secondary school pupils showed an increase in the number of “fake Insta” pages
These news compounded fears that there are sexual content published on them
Twenty percent of the pupils said they had a "major" account they show their parents
One in five children — some as young as 11 years old — sets up accounts on social networks he doesn’t reveal to adults.
A survey of 20,000 secondary school pupils showed a rapid increase in the number of “fake Insta” accounts — the name refers to Instagram, where you can share photos.
These news has compounded fears that these accounts are used to publish content of a sexual nature.
Twenty-percent of pupils said they run a polite "main" account, which shows their parents, and also has private accounts.
One of the mothers, who accidentally came across the secret side of her 13-year-old daughter, encountered a teen who urged others to "rape her."
A study conducted in independent schools by Digital Awareness UK and Headmasters" and Headmistresses" Conference (HMC) found that 40 percent of pupils between the ages of 11 and 18 run two profiles. Half of them admit to maintaining private accounts.
HMC's president, Mike Buchanan, says, "It's worrying that so many teenagers are creating their places online where parents and teachers can't find them."
Eilidh Doyle will become the "vote of athletes" on the Scottish Athletics board
At an annual general meeting of Scottish Athletics, Eilidh Doyle was elected to the board as non-executive director.
Eilidh Doyle is Scotland’s most successful athlete. President Ian Beattie described this move as a great opportunity for people who manage sports to take advantage of their extensive international experience in the last decade.
"Eilidh enjoys great respect in the communities of Scottish, British and global athletics, and we are confident that introducing it to the board will bring great benefits to Scotch athletics," Beattie said.
Doyle said: "I'm very pleased to have the opportunity to convey the voice of athletes to the council, and I'm hoping to be able to really help manage the sport in Scotland."
