He tweeted: "A month ago I had a stroke.
I couldn't walk.
Doctors said that time would tell if I would regain my fitness and to what extent.
It was a catering job, but I fully recovered, learned to walk again and went back to training!
“Thank you for all your words of support!”
Lactators’ advertising, which compares mothers to cows, arouses extreme emotions on the Internet
The breastfeeding company sparked a storm online by posting an advertisement that compares breastfeeding mothers to milked cows.
In order to announce the launch of "the world's first silent breastweaker to wear under clothing," its producer, Elvie, has published a mocking advertisement in the form of a music video that emphasizes the freedom it provides to mothers.
It depicts four real mothers dancing in a hay-filled barn for a piece in which you can hear the words:  “Yes, I milk, but you don’t see the tail” and “As if you didn’t notice, it’s not mentions but my boobs.”
The chorus goes like this: “Pump them out, pump them out, feed them to the children, pump them out, pump them out, I milk their ladies.”
Advertising, which was published on the company’s Facebook page, caused a lot of controversy on the Internet.
The video currently has 77,000 views and hundreds of comments. Users express extreme opinions, many of them saying that it is inappropriate to joke about the “horror” that takes place in the dairy industry.
“Using cows to advertise this product was a very bad idea.
Like people, in order to give milk, they have to get pregnant and give birth to a calf, except that their children are taken away after a few days from birth.”
Elvie breast pump can be worn unnoticed under the feeding bra (Elvie/Mother)
Another person commented: "It is, of course, traumatic for both the mother and the baby.
Sure, let’s use cows to advertise breast pumps for mothers who can keep their children.”
Someone else added: "This advertisement is completely detached from reality."
Others are defending the clip — one of the women said she thought the song was “funny.”
“I think it’s a brilliant idea.
If I were still breastfeeding, I'd buy one.
I felt like a cow.
Advertising is a little crazy, but I'm not looking for a second bottom in it.
It's a brilliant invention," someone wrote.
“It’s a functival advertisement aimed at mothers who pull away food (often at work or toilets) and feel like ‘cows.’
It is not intended to praise or criticize the dairy industry.”
At the end of the film, it turns out that the women danced with breast pumps attached discreetly to their breasts.
The campaign was inspired by the notion that many nursing women feel like cows.
Elvie's breast pump, however, it's completely quiet, there's no cables or tubes and you can wear it unnoticed under a feeding bra, which gives women the freedom to move, hold children on their hands and even leave the house while pulling food away.
Ana Balarin, partner and creative director at Mother, said, “Elvie’s lactator is such a revolutionary product that he deserved a bold and provocative premiere.
By juxtaposing nursing women and dairy cows, we wanted to show all the challenges associated with extracting food, while presenting in a funny and coherent way an incredible sense of freedom that it provides.
This isn't the first time Elvie's breastfall has come to the forefronts.
During the London Fashion Week during Marta Jakubowski’s catwalk there was a mother of two children with this product attached.
Hundreds of immigrant children have been secretly transferred to a tent camp on the Texas border
The number of detained immigrant children is skyrocketing, although the number of people crossing the border every month doesn’t actually change. This is partly due to the fact that strict rhetorics and rules introduced by President Trump's administration have made it difficult to place children at sponsors.
The majority of sponsors are usually undocumented immigrants who are afraid of being exposed to deportation if they apply for a child.
Such a risk increased in June when federal authorities announced that potential sponsors and other adults living on the common farm would be taking fingerprints, which would then be passed on to immigration offices.
Last week, a senior official at Immigration and Customs Enforcement Matthew Albence testified before Congress that his agency had already arrested dozens of people who volunteered as sponsors for unaccompanied children.
The agency later confirmed that 70 per cent of those arrested hadn't been previously listed.
“Nearly 80 percent of people who are sponsors or residing with them in one household stay illegally, and a large part of them are foreigners with a criminal past.
That's why we're constantly after them," Albence said.
Employees of the shelter say that, as part of efforts to speed up the regulation of the children’s circumstances, officials introduced new rules that require some of them to appear in court within a month of detention – and not after 60 days, as was the case according to previous standards.
There will be a lot set up through video conferencing, rather than personally asking an immigration referee to legalize their status.
Those who do not qualify for dismissal will be deported immediately.
According to shelter employees and reports that have been published in recent months, the longer the children are in custody, the easier they can become restless or fall into depression, which in turn can lead to outbursts of aggression or escape attempts.
Defenders have found that these problems are even more serious for larger resorts such as Tornillo, where, due to its size, it is easier to overlook the signals that something bad is happening with the child.
They also added that transferring children to a tent camp without giving them adequate time to prepare emotionally or saying goodbye to their friends can intensify the trauma that many of them are already struggling with.
Syria urges U.S., French and Turkish "occupation forces" to withdraw immediately
During an appearance before the U.N. General Assembly, Syria's foreign minister, Walid al-Moualem, also urged refugees from Syria to return home, even though the war in the country is going on for the eighth year.
Moualem, who also acts as deputy prime minister, said foreign forces were on Syrian soil illegally, under the pretext of combating terrorism, and "we will deal with them appropriately."
“They must withdraw immediately and unconditionally,” he announced to the Congregation.
Moualem claims that "the war on terrorism is almost oven" in Syria, where more than 360,000 people have been killed since 2011 and millions have been forced to flee their homes.
He says Damascus will continue to "lead its holy battle until clearing Syrian territory" from both terrorist groups and "any foreigners staying on it illegally."
There are currently about 2,000 U.S. troops stationed in Syria, who are mainly involved in training Kurdish forces and Syrians opposing President Bashar al-Assad and advising them.
In addition, there are more than 1,000 French soldiers in a war-ravaged country.
When it comes to refugees, Moualem stated that conditions are good enough for them to come back, and accused "some Western countries" of "raising irrational fear," which discourages them from returning.
“We asked the international community and humanitarian organizations to help these returns,” he said.
“They politicize matters that should have a purely human dimension.”
The United States and the European Union have warned that as long as President Assad does not enter into a political agreement with the opposition to end the war, Syria will not receive any aid for reconstruction.
U.N. diplomats say the latest agreement between Russia and Turkey on the establishment of a buffer zone in the last major rebel bastion in Idlib has created an opportunity to continue political talks.
The treaty between Russia and Turkey has helped prevent a major attack by Syrian forces backed by Russia on a province where three million people live.
Moualem stressed, however, that the deal had a "clear timeframe," and expressed hope that military action would target jihadists, including militanters from the al-Qaeda-linked Levant Civil Defense Front, which would be "eliminated."
The U.N. envoy, Staffan de Mistura, hopes to convene the first meetings of a new commission made up of members of the ruling party and the opposition in order to develop a sketch of Syria's post-war constitution and prepare for elections.
Moualem outlined the conditions for the Syrian government's participation in the commission, saying that the panel's work should be limited "to verifying the provisions of the current constitution" and warned against interference.
Why Trump will win the election again
According to this logic, President Trump would have been elected for a second term in 2020, unless, as many liberal observers probably hoppes, his presidency would sooner end in office removal and scandal.
It would undoubtedly be "the most dramatic finale of the presidency so far."
So far, there are no signs of viewer fatigue.
Since 2014, CNN's primate tim's viewerships have doubled and reached 1.05 million viewers, and the MSNBC channel has almost tripled, to 1.6 million viewers.
According to Nielsen, Fox News attracts an average of 2.4 million viewers during the highest viewership hours, an increase from 1.7 million years ago, and MSNBC’s “The Rachel Maddow Show” program achieved the highest scores among cable television, attracting as many as 3.5 million viewers of evening news programs.
“Humans fly like moths to fire — that’s something we don’t understand,” said Neal Bear, chief producer of the ABC series “Designated Survivor” about the secretary of the cabinet, which after an attack on Capitol becomes president.
Nell Scovell, an experienced comedy writer and author of "Just the Funny Parts: And a Few Hard Truths About Sneaking Into the Hollywood Boys" Club, has another theory.
She mentions how she took a taxi in Boston before the 2016 election.
The driver said he would vote for Trump.
“Why?” she asked.
"Because he'll make me laugh," he replied.
“Chaos has an entertainment value.”
Of course, reports from Washington could, like nothing else shown on television, determine the future of Roe v. Wade’s case, about the admissibility of merging immigrant families or about the state of the global economy.
Exclusion is a luxury that only the most privileged viewers can afford.
Besides, it’s not just about being a well-informed citizen when it’s the sixth hour you’ve been watching a panel of experts debating Bob Woodward’s use of secret informants while writing the book “Fear,” Paul Manafort’s ostrich-skinned jacket for $15,000 (“clothes overloaded with pride,” as The Washington Post put it out), or the implications of the president’s shock details
For example, I will never look at Super Mario like I did before.
“What he’s doing is in part reminiscent of a reality show in that it delivers new content every night,” said Brent Montgomery, CEO of Wheelhouse Entertainment and creator of “Pawn Stars.”
You can't afford to miss an episode because you'll be in arrears.
When I met this week with Mike Fleiss, the weather on the north coast of Kauai Island, where he has his home, was beautiful, but he was sitting inside, watching MSNBC and recording CNN at the same time.
He couldn't get away from it, not when Brett Kavanaugh was standing before the Senate Judiciary Committee and weighing on the future of the Supreme Court.
“I rememember how we used to do all these crazy programs, and people used to say ‘it’s the beginning of the end of Western civilization,” Fleiss told me.
“I thought they were joking, but it turns out they were right.”
