Influx of money from the outside for the congressional election campaign
It is not surprising that after the reorganization of constituencies, as a result of which two current congressmens now stand in the race for one position, a river of cash flows into the 17th electoral constituency in Pennsylvania.
Pittsburg’s recently created suburban constituency is represented in Congress by a representative of the Democrats, Conor Lamb, who obtained his place in another constituency during special elections last spring.
Lamb competes with another current congressman, Republican Keith Rothfus now representing the former 12th district in Pennsylvania, which largely coincides with the new district 17.
District maps were corrected after the Pennsylvania Supreme Court's January verdict, which ruled that the old districts were designated dishonestly, which gave an advantage to the Republicans and was unconstitutional.
The race in the new constituence 17 began with a fight to finance campaigns between the party’s main funding bodies — the Democratic Congressional Committee (DCCC) and the National Republican Campaign Committee (NRCC).
Lamba's name gained popularity in Pennsylvania after winning by a hair in a widely commented election to the 18th constituency held in March.
The Republican has been in this position for more than a decade, and Donald Trump has won in this district by 20 points.
Policy experts gave Democrats a slight advantage.
The United States considered punishing El Salvador for supporting China, but withdrew from the idea
Diplomats noted that the Dominicans and Panama had already acknowledged Beijing, despite Washington's slight opposition.
In June 2017, President Donald Trump held a cordial meeting with President of Panama, Juan Carlos Varela, and by the time the Trump Organization’s management team was expelled in Panama.
State Department officials have decided to recall U.S. chief diplomatic missions from El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama because of "recent decisions to stop recognizing Taiwan," as department spokesman Heather Nauert said in a statement this month.
However, the sanctions under consideration were to be imposed only on El Salvador, which, according to estimates in 2017, received 140 million dollars of aid from the United States, among others, for the fight against drugs and support for development and the economy.
The proposed sanctions, which were supposed to cover restrictions on financial assistance and the granting of visas to certain individuals, would be very severe for this Central American country, which is struggling with high unemployment and a high murder rate.
As international meetings progressed, North and Central American officials postponed a high-level conference aimed at security and economic well-being to address proposals from a similar assembly from last year that was seen as a step forward in efforts to prevent immigrants from entering the United States.
In mid-September, top administration officials made it clear that they wanted the conference to take place, thus cutting off all considerations of sanctions against El Salvador.
Diplomats say that now at the conference, which is currently scheduled for mid-October, Vice President Mike Pence is expected to appear, which is expected to be a signal of inviting representatives of the administration.
Three U.S. envoys returned quietly to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic. This was not accompanied by any new strict communications or punishments from Washington.
A White House spokesperson for John Bolton declined to comment on the details of the debate, which were put forward by three U.S. officials, including two diplomats who, subject to anonymity, agreed to discuss internal negotiations.
Their accounts were confirmed by an external analyst from the environment of the administration, who also spoke anonymously.
Learn history
Another element may be a report by special prosecutor Robert Mueller on the possible obstruction of justice action by President Trump, for which there is currently concrete evidence available to the public.
According to reports, Robert Mueller's investigation is also being investigated whether Donald Trump's campaign had anything to do with Russia's attack on U.S. elections.
If the line-up of Congress changes, President Trump will be held accountable by him in preparation for a re-appearance before voters, and perhaps even be tried before the jury.
There are a lot of unknowns here, and I don’t want to suggest that Trump’s downfall — or those in his equal positions in Europe — is inevitable.
All of us on both sides of the Atlantic have to make a choice that decides how much longer this can last.
In 1938, German officers were ready to carry out a couple against Hitler, if only the West had resisted him and supported Czechoslovakia in Munich.
It didn't work out and we've lost the opportunity to unleash the years of carnage that followed.
The turns of history revolve around such turning points, accelerating or slowing down the inevitable process of democracy.
There are now a few such turning points in front of the Americans.
What do we do if Donald Trump fires Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, the man on whom the future of Robert Mueller's investigation depends?
Rosenstein has been in Trouble since we revealed last year that he had proposed a secret recording of the president last year and speculated that he wasn't fit for office.
According to Rosenstein, the account presented by The Times is inaccurate.
“How do we react if the FBI’s newly commissioned investigation into Brett Kavanaugh proves incomplete or unfaithful, or if he is appointed as a Supreme Court jedge, despite his credible accusations of sexual assault and making false statements?
And above all – will we elect people who will hold Donald Trump accountable in the middle of the presidential term?
If we don't go through these trials, there will be tough times for democracy.
However, I believe that we will succeed – due to the lesson I received in Prague.
My mother was a Czechoslovak Jew. She was deported to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime that once occupied my ambassador's residence.
Survived, emigrated to America and 60 years later sent me to ignite Sabbath candles on that table marked with a swastika.
“Having such a legacy, how could I not look to the future optimistically?”
Norman Eisen, a senior research associate at the Brookings Institution, is the president of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington, D.C., and the author of "The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House."
Rangers club player Graham Dorrans is optimistically awaitinging for a match against Rapid Vienna
Rangers are takinging Rapid Vienna on Thursday with the knowledge that victory against the Austrians, obtained after an impressive draw with Villareal reached this month in Spain, will significantly increase their chances of qualifying from the G of the Europa League.
Up to a 2:2 draw with Villareal's knee injury prevented midfielder Graham Dorrans from playing this season, but he believes this result will work on the Rangers like a springboard to greater success.
"It's done well to us because Villarreal is in good shape," the 31-year-old said.
“We went to this match with the belief that we were going to achieve something — and won a point.
Perhaps in the end we could still push them, but in general the draw is probably a fair result.
Spaniards were probably better in the first half, but in the second it was us who were better.
As for Thursday, it's another important evening for Europe.
We hope that we will be able to score three points, but this match will be difficult, because our opponent got a good result in his last match. However, I am sure that thanks to the support of the fans, we will go through this and we will win a positive result.
