Federal prosecutors are demanding the death penalty for a suspected terrorist attack in New York
Federal prosecutors from New York are seeking the death penalty for Sayfull Saipov suspected of carrying out a terrorist attack in New York that left eight people dead. This is a rare punishment — it has not been carried out in this state for a federal offence since 1953.
To carry out an attack that took place in October on a bike path running along the West Side Highway in Lower Manhattan, thirty-something Saipov allegedly used a rented Home Depot truck, which pedestrians and cyclists ran over on his way.
According to a notification filed in New York's Southern District of New York about its intention to demand the death penalty to justify its necessity, prosecutors will have to prove that Saipov "deliberately" killed eight victims and "intentionally" caused serious bodily injury.
According to the court document, for both of these charges, they face the death penalty.
A few weeks after an attack by a federal juror, it presented Saipov with an indictment covering 22 positions, including eight counts of murder as part of illegal activity — a procedure typically used by federal prosecutors in organized crime cases — and an allegation of violence and the destruction of motor vehicles.
According to prosecutors, orchestrating the attack required “solid planning and rethinking.” The way Saipov carried it out described it as “monstrous, cruel and immoral.”
“Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov brought harm, damage and loss to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza, and Alejandra Damian Pagnucca.”
Five of the victims were Argentine nationals who stayed in New York tourism.
The last time for the death penalty in New York District was filed ten years ago.
Defendant Khalid Barnes was found guilty of murdering two drug dealers, but eventually in September 2009 he heard a life sentence.
The death penalty as a result of a federal case was last carried out in New York in 1953 on Julius and Ethel Rosenbergs. The marriage was executed after two years earlier they were found guilty of conspiracy and espionage to the Soviet Union during the Cold War.
Both Rosenbergs were executed in an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
According to court documents, Saipov, which is native to Uzbekistan, in the months after the attack showed no remorse.
Police say he told investigators he felt good about what he had done.
According to the indictment, Saipov confessed to the authorities that he was inspired by ISIS videos that he watched on his phone.
According to police, he also demanded that an ISIS flag be displayed in his hospital room.
An indictment involving 22 charges responds that he is innocent.
David Patton, one of the federal public defenders who represent Saipova, said they were "obviously disappointed" with the prosecutor's decision.
“We believe that the decision to demand the death penalty and the rejection of a life sentence without the possibility of early dismissal after a guilty plea will only prolong the suffering these events have reduced to all those concerned,” he said.
Saipov's defense team had previously asked prosecutors not to demand the death penalty.
Tory MP says NIGEL FARAGE should take responsibility for Brexit negotiations
During today's protest, which took place during the Tories conference, Nigel Farage vowed "to mobilize an army of the people."
The former leader of the United Kingdom Independence Party (UKIP) said politicians must "feel hot" beating from Eurosceptics when one of Theresa May's parliamentarians suggested he should take responsibility for negotiations with the European Union.
Conservative MP Peter Bone told demonstrators in Birmingham that if Farage had been secretary for Brexit, "the United Kingdom would already be outside the EU."
However, the addition of Tories in favour of remaining in the community for a separate protest against Brexit held in the city further highlighted the burden of reconciling its deeply divided ranks facing Theresa May.
The Prime Minister is struggling to implement his Chequers trade-of-compromise plan among attacks by Brexit supporters, his opponents and the European Union itself.
Its allies say that, despite her objections, she will seek to conclude a deal with Brussels and force Eurosceptics and Labour to make a choice between her proposal and "chaos."
Peter Bone told the Leave Means Leave in Solihull that he wanted to "throw the plan out of Chequers."
He suggested that Nigel Farage should become the proxy, who should be handed over responsibility for the negotiations with Brussels.
“If he were into it, we’d be outside the EU now,” he said.
The Wellingborough MP added: "I support Brexit, but we have to throw the plan out of Chequers."
Presenting his opposition to the European Union, he said: "This is not why we fought in world wars to be subject to someone now.
We want to be our rights in our own country.”
Peter Bone declined the suggestion that citizens have changed their minds since the 2016 vote: "Thinking British people have changed their mind and want to stay in the Union is completely untrue."
Andrea Jenkyns of the Tory party, who told reporters: “I’m just saying: “Prime Minister, please listen to the people.
The Chequers plan does not enjoy the support of the general public, the opposition will not vote for it, our party or our activists who populate the streets and who have chosen us do not support it either.
“Give up the plan from Chequers and start listening.”
In a statement addressed to Prime Minister May, she added: "The prime ministers maintain their positions as they keep their promises."
Nigel Farage stated that the politicians taking part in the demonstrations must "feel hot" if they were to betray the decision taken in the 2016 referendum.
“Now it’s a question of trust between us — the people — and the political class,” he said.
“They’re trying to betray Brexit, and we’re here today to tell them ‘we won’t let you do.”
Turning to the enthusiastic crowd, he added: "I want you to make politicians who are on the brink of treacherous Brexit feel hot.
“We are mobilizing an army of the people of this country that has given us victory in the Brexit referendum, and we will not rest until we become an independent, independent, proud United Kingdom.”
At the time, opponents of Brexit marched through the city to the center where a two-hour demonstration took place.
A handful of activists were waving "Tory's banners against Brexit" after the group was formed this weekend.
Labour activist Baron Andrew Adonis mocked the Conservatives for issues with the security of the party app at the start of the conference.
“These are people who say they can organize information systems and all the technology to support the Canada plan plus plus, a liquid border, free trade without borders with Ireland,” he added.
“It’s a complete farce.
There is no such thing as a good Brexit," he added.
