Giants, who have lost for their last five matches, will play the next match at home at Milton Keynes on Friday.
An air traffic controller dies helping hundreds of people on the plane escape the quake
An air traffic controller from Indonesia has been hailed as a hero after he was killed, helping to safely take off the plane with hundreds of people on board.
More than 8,800 people were killed and many were found missing after a major quake that struck the Indonesian island of Celebes on Friday, triggering a tsunami.
There are still strong aftershocks in the area. Many people have been trapped in debris in the city of Palu.
Even though his colleagues were fleeing, saving their lives, 21-year-old Anthonius Gunawan Agung refused to leave his position in the lucking tower of flight control at Mutiara Sis Al Jufri airport in Palu.
He stayed there to ensure that the Batik Air (flight number 6321), which was just on the runway, took off safely.
He then jumped out of the tower when he thought he was about to collapse.
He died in hospital.
According to Australian ABC News, Air Viationa spokesman Yohannes Sirait said the controller's decision probably saved the lives of hundreds of people.
We've set up a helicopter from the town of Balikpapan in Borneo to move it to a larger hospital in another city.
Unfortunately, Anthonius passed away this morning, before the helicopter reached Palu.
“This message has torn our hearts,” he added.
Meanwhile, authorities are worried that the death toll could reach thousands. The country's disaster relief agency reports the possibility of commuting to Donggala, Sigi and Boutong cities is limited.
“We believe that the toll of death continues to increase because many of the bodies are still under debris and can’t be reached,” Sutopo spokesman Purwo Nugroho said.
Tsunami waves stretching back from six metres destroyed Palu, where the mass funeral of the victims will take place on Sunday.
Military and civilian planes provide assistance and supplies.
Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, told Sky News: "Ambulances are constantly bringing bodies.
There's a shortage of drinking water.
Small shops are plundered.”
Jan Gelfand, head of the International Red Cross in Indonesia, told CNN: "The Indonesian Red Cross is rushing to help survivors, but we don't know what we'll find at the scene.
It’s already a tragedy, and it could be even worse.”
On Sunday, Indonesia’s president, Joko Widodo, arrived in Palu: “I ask all of you to work day and night to complete all the evacuation tasks.
“Are you ready?”
This year, Indonesia was already struck by an earthquake that killed more than 550 people on Lombok Island.
