Postcards from bereaved women are meant to help prevent suicide on the bridge
Two women whose loved ones have committed suicide are trying to dissuade others from their intentions to take their own lives.
Sharon Davis and Kelly Humphreys put postcards on the Welsh bridge with inspiring messages and phone numbers where people can get help.
Mrs. Davis's son, Tyler, was 13 when he contracted depression. At the age of 18, he took his own life.
“I don’t want any parent to feel the way I feel every day,” he said.
Sharon Davis, 45, who lives in Lydney, recounts that her son was a well-promising chef with a contagious smile.
“Everyone knew his smile.
They have always said that he can brighten any room with it.”
Before his death, however, he gave up his job because he was "in a really black place."
In 2014, Tyler’s brother was 11 years old, and it was he who found his brother when he committed suicide.
Ms Davis says, “I’m still afraid of the domino effect.”
Sharon Davis created the cards “to tell people that there are people who can be turned to and talked to, even if it’s just a friend.
“Don’t sit in silence – talk to someone.”
Kelly Humphreys, who has been friends with Sharon Davies for years, soon after her mother's death, has lost Mark, her partner for 15 years.
"He didn't say he felt depressed, sad or anything," he says.
“A few days before Christmas, we noticed a change in his behavior.
At Christmas, he was already in a complete hole. When the children opened the presents, he didn’t even look at them.”
