Under Kim's rule, the number of North Korean fugitives is declining
According to South Korean legislators since Kim Jong-un took power seven years ago, the number of people fleeing North Korea has fallen to South.
Quoting data from the South Korean Unification Ministry, Park Byeong-seug said there were 1127 escapes last year, and in 2011 — 2706.
According to him, the key factors that affected this situation are more accurate controls at the border between North Korea and China, and higher rates charged by human smugglers.
Pyongyang did not issue a public statement on the matter.
The vast majority of North Korean escapees are ultimately South Korea's proposed citizenship.
According to authorities in Seulu, more than 30,000 North Koreans have crossed the border illegally since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most are fleeing through China, which has the longest border with North Korea, making it easier to cross it than a tightly protected demilitarized zone (DMZ) between the both Koreas.
China sees refugees as illegal immigrants, not refugees, and often forcibly sends them back to the country.
Relations between the North and the South, which are theoretically still at war, in recent months have clearly improved.
This month, the leaders of the two countries met in Pyongyang, where they held talks on negotiations on denuclearization, which had previously stalled.
This happened after a historic meeting between the president of the United States, Donald Trump, with Kim Jong-un, held in Singapore in June. The result of this meeting is a general agreement on the quest to cleanse the Korean peninsula of nuclear weapons.
However, North Korea's foreign minister, Ri Yong-ho, accused the United States of failing progress on the issue.
“Without trust in the United States, there will be no certainty about our national security, and in these circumstances there is no possibility that we will unilaterally disarm ourselves first,” Ri said in a speech he gave before the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
Nancy Pelosi calls Brett Kavanaugh a "hysterical" and says he's not fit to work in the Supreme Court
Minority leader in the House of Representatives, Nancy Pelosi, named Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh "hysterical" and said she was inherently unfit to work in the Supreme Court.
Pelosi delivered these words during an interview she gave Saturday at the Texas Tribune Festival in Austin.
“I think that if a woman behaved in this way, she’d be considered a ‘hysterical,’” Pelosi said of her reaction to the testimony Kavanaugh was giving on Thursday before the Senate Justice Committee.
Kavanaugh emotionally denied allegations of alleged sexual harassing Dr Christina Blasey Ford when they were both teenagers.
At times during his entrance speech, Kavanaugh showed strong emotions—he was almost screaming and losing his speech when discussing family issues and high school years.
He also rebuked the Democrats clearly from the committee, detailing his allegations of a "grotesque and coordinated swing on reputation," organized by liberals, furious that Hillary Clinton lost the 2016 presidential election.
Pelosi stated that according to her, Kavanaugh’s testimony showed that he was not fit to work in the Supreme Court because it revealed his prejudices against the Democrats.
“I think he disqualified himself by making these statements, as well as by the way he attacked the Clintons and Democrats,” she said.
When asked if she'd try to dismiss Kavanaugh from office if he had received the nomination and if the Democrats had won a majority in the House of Representatives, Pelosi hesitated.
“I’ll say this: if he doesn’t tell the truth to Congress or the FBI, he shouldn’t only sit on the Supreme Court, he’s also supposed to be in office in the court where he’s currently working,” she said.
Kavanaugh is currently a judge in a district court of appeal in the District of Columbia.
Pelosi added that as a Democrat, she fears Kavanaugh's possible rulings on the Affordable Care Act or Roe v. Wade's trial because he is regarded as a Conservative judge.
During a hearing confirming Kavanaugh, he bypassed questions as to whether he would repeal certain Supreme Court decisions.
“This is not the time to bring a hysterical, prejudiced person into court, who will expect us to say “it’s wonderful,” Pelosi added.
And women have to wield it.
It's the right diatribe, the months, and the years of spilling fury, and she can't throw it away without crying.
“We’re crying when we’re furious,” Gloria Steinem told me 45 years later.
“It’s quite frequent, isn’t it?”
She added: “I was greatly helped by a woman who was a manager in some company. She said that she also cries when she’s furious, but she came up with such a way that when she gets nervous and starts crying, she talks to the person she’s talking to ’You probably think I’m sad because I’m crying.
I'm furious."
And he's still doing his thing.
I thought it was brilliant.”
Tears are permissible as an outlet of wrath in part because they are basically misunderstood.
One of my clear memories of one of my first jobs in a men-dominated office is how one time I started crying out of unspeakable rage. An elderly woman — a cool executive I’ve always been a little scared — grabbed me by the neck at the time and led me to the staircase.
“Never let them see you cry,” she said.
“They don’t know you’re angry.
They think they're sad, and they're glad they've baked them."
Patricia Schroeder of the Democratic Party, the then congressional deputy from Colorado, worked with Gary Hart on his presidential campaign.
In 1987, when Hart was caught with a mistress on a boat called Monkey Business and withdrew from the race, frustrated to the borders, Patricia realized that she could properly consider the idea of running for president on his own.
"It wasn't a well-thought-out decision," she told me, laughing, 30 years later.
“There were already seven other candidates until the election, and the last thing they needed was another.
“Someone described the situation as ‘Snāšké and the seven dwarves.’”
As the campaign had been going on for a long time, Schroeder had a backlog of fundraising and promised herself that she wouldn't join the race if she didn't raise 2 million dollars.
It was a battle doomed to lose.
At the time, she discovered that some of her supporters, who gave the men 1000 dollars, had only 250 for her.
“Do they think I have any discounts?” she wondered.
During a speech in which she announced that she would not run a formal campaign, she was so filled with emotions — gratitude to the people who supported her, the frustration of a system that made it so difficult to raise money and turn to voters rather than delegates, and the rage to sexism — that the voice tied in her throat.
"You'd think I'd had a nervous breakdown," the press said.
“You’d think I’d be sponsored by Kleenex.
I remember wondering what they'd write on my tombstone.
“Cried?”
What the economic war between the United States and China could bring to Beijing
Salvos starting an economic war between the U.S. and China have been stunning, and although the battle is still going on, in the long run the conflict between these countries may be beneficial for Beijing, experts say.
The first warning this year was fired by U.S. President Donald Trump, imposing tariffs on China's key export goods, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation occurred this week after the introduction of new tariffs on US$200 billion (US$150-billion pounds), which resulted in the taxation of half of all goods coming into the U.S
Each time Beijing reciprocated in nature, recently raising tariffs on U.S. goods worth 60 billion dollars from five to ten percent.
China is determined to respond to every U.S. strike, and it doesn't look like the world's other economy is about to step down.
Forcing Washington to withdraw means accepting demands, but a public tilt before the United States would be too embarrassing for China's president, Xi Jinping.
According to experts, however, if Beijing plays its cards well, the pressure from a trade war with the U.S. could, in the long run, strengthen China, reducing the dependencies between the two economies.
“The fact that a quick political decision made in Washington or Beijing could create conditions for unleashing economic chaos in one of the countries is, in fact, much more dangerous than observers have admitted so far,” said Abigail Grace, an Asia researcher at the think tank Center for New American Security.
According to the foreign minister, Syria is "ready" for the return of refugees
The government says Syria is ready for the voluntary return of refugees, and asks for help in rebuilding the country ruined as a result of more than seven years of war.
In his speech before the U.N. General Assembly, Syria's foreign minister Walid al-Moualem stated that the country's conditions are improving.
"The situation on our territory is now more stable and safe thanks to advances in combating terrorism," he said.
Striving to restore normality, the government is constantly rebuilding areas destroyed by terrorists.
All conditions have already been met so that refugees, who had to leave the country due to terrorism and unilateral economic moves aimed at their daily lives and livelihoods, can voluntarily return to it.
According to U.N. estimates, more than 5.5 million citizens have left Syria since the start of the war in 2011.
Another six million people who still live there need humanitarian aid.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian regime would be happy to accept assistance in rebuilding the destroyed country.
However, he stressed that he would not accept aid fortified by the conditions or offered by the countries that sponsored the rebellion.
Europe seals victory at Ryder Cup in Paris
The European team won Ryder Cup 2018, beating the U.S
On European soil, the United States lost for the sixth time in a row and has not won the Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe regained its trophy when, under the lead of the Danish captain Thomas Bjorn, it scored 14.5 points that were needed to defeat the U.S.
The American star, Phil Mickelson, who didn't do very well for most of the tournament, hicked the ball from the stand straight into the water at the 16th PAR3 hole, thus giving the match to Francesca Molinari.
The Italian golfer Molinari shone throughout all the rounds and became one of the four players who, since 1979, when this tournament was first held in the current format, managed to get a score of 5-0-0.
American Jordan Spieth has been eliminated by the lowest-ranked player from Europe, Thorbjorn Olesen of Denmark.
Dustin Johnson, which ranks highest in the world rankings, lost two hollows ahead of the final hole with Englishman Ian Poulter, for whom it may have been Ryder Cup's last tournament.
The European with the highest number of victories in Ryder Cup was Spaniard Sergio Garcia, who has already taken part in eight such tournaments and gained 25.5 career points.
“I usually don’t cry, but I can’t help myself today.
It's been a tough year.
I'm very grateful to Thomas for believing in me and choosing me.
I'm very happy to get the cup back.
It’s the success of the whole team and I’m glad that I was able to help,” Garcia said after the team’s victory.
He passes the baton to his compatriot John Rahm, which beat an American golfer legend, Tiger Woods, 2&1 in a singles game.
“I feel incredible pride in winning against Tiger Woods. I grew up watching his game,” Rahm, 23, said.
Woods have lost all matches in France and are nowed to score 13-21-3 in the Ryder Cup.
It's an unprecedented statistic for one of the greatest players of all time, who won the 14 most important titles and gave way only to Jack Nicklaus.
The U.S. team has had trouble finding a treadmill all weekend, except Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who have played at a really high level throughout the tournament.
After a disappointing performance by the U.S. line-up, his captain Jim Furyk said: "I'm proud of the guys, they fought.
At some point this morning, we pressed some Europeans.
We've withdrawn.
Hats off in front of Thomas.
He's a great captain.
All of his 12 players played really well.
We’re going to change the line-up of the group. Together with PGA of America and our Ryder Cup Commission, we’ll find a way to move forward.
I love these 12 guys and I'm proud to be their captain.
I'm full of admiration for my opponents.
“They’ve played much better than us.”
Information about the bloom — in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota, the density of plankton decreases
The Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission's latest report shows an overall decline in plankton density in some parts of Tampa Bay.
According to the FWC, the presence of mosque blooms has been reported in Pinellas counties, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier, suggesting that its density decreases.
It stretches over a length of about 209 kilometers of the coastline, from the north of Pinellas County to the nooth of Lee County.
Eruptions can be encountered about 16 kilometers from the coasts of Hillsbourgo County, but compared with the previous week in fewer places.
Blossom has also been observed in Pasco County.
Last week there was an average concentration of plankton off the coast and waters of Pinellas County, low to medium off the coast of Hillsborough County, natural to the high in Manatee County, natural to the coast high and in Sarasota county waters, natural to the average in Charlotte County, natural to high off the coast and in the waters of the county of Lee County.
Cases of respiratory irritation are still being reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Last week, however, they weren't recorded in northwest Florida.
